window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"p": "index.html", "t": "First-principles conductance of a 1D Hf₂Se₉–VSe₃ heterojunction\n      working", "x": "First-principles conductance of a 1D Hf₂Se₉–VSe₃ heterojunction working Members: Seongbin Park · Young Woo Choi (advisor) A single Hf₂Se₉ molecule bridges two metallic VSe₃ chains, forming a 1D molecular junction grown inside a carbon nanotube. The project computes the junction's conductance from first principles and asks one physical question: does current flow through the molecule, and through which orbital? The transport evidence says yes — removing the molecule kills the conductance entirely — and the present frontier is correcting the molecular level positions (PBE underestimates the gap) so the resonance sits where it physically should. The junction: a Hf₂Se₉ molecule (cyan Hf, orange Se) bridges two H-passivated VSe₃ chains (grey V, orange Se) acting as 1D electrodes. Central region of the relaxed 220-atom transport device (vdW-DF2, 60° staggered); transport axis horizontal. Idea VSe₃ forms a 1D chain; among the trichalcogenides its coordination had no experimental report, and here it is identified as trigonal prismatic (TP) and is metallic — a natural 1D electrode. Encapsulating a Hf₂Se₉ molecule between two such chains makes a metal–molecule–metal junction. Because the molecule's HOMO–LUMO gap is tiny in PBE (0.024 eV), its frontier resonance sits almost on the Fermi level and transmission looks \"too good.\" Whether the headline picture is resonant or non-resonant tunnelling therefore hinges on getting the molecular level alignment right. Method DFT (SIESTA / VASP): chain and junction relaxation, band structure, PDOS. Production functional is vdW-DF2 — PBE+D2 was anomalously unstable for the Hf₂Se₉ relaxation and was dropped ( 02. Hf₂Se₉ stability ). Transport (TranSIESTA / TBtrans): ~220-atom zero-bias NEGF, electrode k = 100, broadening η = 0.005 eV. Transmission T(E), PDOS, and eigenchannels (Re/Im/|ψ|² cubes + z-profile). Molecular-level correction (DFT+Σ): orbital shifts $\\Delta_i=\\varepsilon_i^{\\text{B3LYP}}-\\varepsilon_i^{\\text{vdW-DF2}}$ from the vacuum molecule, applied to the device Hamiltonian as a projector $\\Delta H_{\\mu\\nu}=\\sum_i \\Delta_i\\,(C^i_\\mu)^{*}C^i_\\nu$ on the Hf/Se block — implemented through TBtrans's built-in δH, no source patch. Status Structure is settled (vdW-DF2 relaxation; 60° staggered registry favoured by 43.3 meV). Transport is converged: scattering-region length is converged, k-grid and η converged, and the three-way comparison pristine 0.70 / heterojunction 0.35 / disconnected 0 for $T(E_F)$ proves the molecule is the sole conduction bridge (eigenchannel $\\tau_0 = 0.999$). The DFT+Σ molecular-level correction is applied ($\\Delta_\\text{HOMO}=-1.015$, $\\Delta_\\text{LUMO}=-0.286$ eV; gap 0.02 → 0.75 eV): on the 9-cell device $T(E_F)$ drops $0.355\\to0.051$ and the channel moves to the HOMO resonance. The live task is the finite-bias I–V with the corrected Hamiltonian and charge-transfer $\\Delta\\rho$. How this site is organized Motivation — the experiment behind this calculation (the starting point). Plan — figure plan and the near-term computation queue. Methods — DFT (vdW-DF2) + NEGF setup and the DFT+Σ correction. Results — the current junction results plus the chronological topic reports that got us here. Log — dated meeting notes."}, {"p": "iterations/index.html", "t": "Iterations", "x": "Iterations 지난 회차 보관 — 사이드바 별도 섹션, 최신순 연구는 여러 회차(iteration)를 돈다. 지금 회차 는 루트(Plan · Methods · Results)에 있고, 끝난 회차 는 여기에 보관한다. 각 회차는 자기 폴더 iterNNN/ 안에 개요(index) + 작업별 서브페이지 로 들어간다 (메인 섹션이 하위 페이지를 갖는 것과 동일). iterations/ iter002/ index.html 이 회차 개요 (목표 · 결과 · 이어진 것) t01-…/ t02-…/ 회차 안의 작업들 — 각각 하위 페이지 iter001/ … 새 회차 시작하는 법 루트의 plan/ methods/ results/ 를 iterations/iterNNN/ 로 이동(동결). 작업이 여럿이면 iterNNN/index.html (개요) + t01-…/ t02-…/ (작업별 서브페이지)로 쪼갠다. 각 회차 index.html 에 <meta name=\"order\" content=\"-NN\"> — 최신 회차가 위로 오게. 루트에서 새로 시작 — 처음부터든, 지난 회차에서 쓸 것만 가져오든. 각 회차는 자기완결 (지난 회차를 가리키지 말고 그 회차만 봐도 이해되게). python3 gen-tree.py — 사이드바 갱신. (아직 보관된 회차 없음. 실제 예시: sogang-qmp/128-gd2c-trap .)"}, {"p": "iterations/iter001-vesper-original/h-termination/index.html", "t": "H termination of the VSe₃ interface: separation minimum restored", "x": "H termination of the VSe₃ interface: separation minimum restored Source: reports/h-termination/ (progress-update.md, h-termination.md, README.md), meeting 2026-04-16. Goal Test the hypothesis (proposed at the 2026-04-07 meeting) that the missing equilibrium in the bare VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉ separation curve is caused by dangling bonds on the cut VSe₃ interface Se . In the TP chain each Se bonds to two V (V–Se = 2.508 Å); cutting the chain removes one V, leaving an unsaturated bond that binds unphysically strongly to Hf₂Se₉. Hf₂Se₉ is closed-shell, so the problem is localized to the VSe₃ side. Verification: attach H to the VSe₃ interface Se and compare the separation-energy curve with the bare case. Method H attached to the 3 interface Se on each side (6 H total), VSe₃ side only. H placed toward the missing-V direction (Se–H bond aligned with the original V–Se direction). d(Se–H) = 1.46 Å (H₂Se experimental r₀, NIST CCCBDB). Single-point scan d = 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 3.5, 4.0, 4.5 Å. vdW-DF2 (LMKLL), MeshCutoff 500 Ry, k = 1×1×4, 44 atoms (6 V + 2 Hf + 30 Se + 6 H). Here d is the z-distance between the VSe₃ terminal Se and the Hf₂Se₉ terminal Se. Result With H, a clear separation-energy minimum appears at d = 3.5 Å — matching the experimental ~3.5 Å. The bare structure keeps decreasing monotonically. d (Å) E sep , with H (eV) E sep , without H (eV) 2.0 +26.90 — 2.5 +4.92 −6.21 3.0 +0.15 −4.10 3.5 −0.68 (min) −1.61 4.0 −0.48 0.00 4.5 0.00 (ref) — \"Without H\" = 08-hetero-v2 (vdW-DF2 symmetric), referenced to d = 4.0 Å. \"With H\" referenced to d = 4.5 Å. Absolute Kohn–Sham energies are in scripts/plot_sep_energy.py . Fig. 1. Separation energy vs interface separation: H-terminated (with H) develops a minimum at d = 3.5 Å (circled); the bare structure (without H) does not. Green band = experimental separation. (Plot: scripts/plot_sep_energy.py .) H-orientation comparison (2026-04-16) Three initial H directions were compared under the same conditions (vdW-DF2 single-point, 44 atoms, Se–H = 1.46 Å): bond (H toward the missing V center), gap (H ⊥ Se₃ plane, i.e. pure ±z), and tilt (H tilted 45° radially outward). gap is the most stable over the whole range; its minimum sits at d = 3.5–4.0 Å (the two points differ by ~4 meV). bond is 1.11 eV higher and tilt 3.72 eV higher than gap . Recommended relaxation starting structure: gap mode, d = 3.5 Å. Fig. 2. Schematic of the three H-orientation modes (bond / gap / tilt). Fig. 3. Separation-energy scan for the three H-orientation modes. Discussion H passivation removes the interface's unphysical strong binding: d < 3.0 Å rises steeply (Pauli repulsion / repulsive wall), d ≈ 3.5 Å balances vdW attraction and repulsion (equilibrium), d > 4.0 Å converges. This indicates the VSe₃ Se dangling bond was the main cause of the earlier separation failure. The H-direction choice is supported by MoS₂-edge literature (Bollinger 2003, Kronberg 2017, Lauritsen 2018): the chalcogen–H bond points along the dangling-bond direction, not toward the metal center; the ~1 eV mode spread is consistent with those studies. Next steps (as stated) Finalize H direction (bond/gap/tilt comparison). Full relaxation at transport size (20uc). Hf₂Se₉ PDOS → band gap. TranSIESTA transport (electrode + device + I-V)."}, {"p": "iterations/iter001-vesper-original/hetero-separation/index.html", "t": "VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉ heterostructure separation test", "x": "VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉ heterostructure separation test Source: reports/initial-structures/failure-analysis.md §6, hetero-energy-vs-sep.csv, meeting 2026-04-07. Goal In the VSe₃(3uc)–Hf₂Se₉–VSe₃(3uc) heterojunction, compute the single-point energy as a function of the VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉ interface separation d (2.5–4.0 Å), to find the equilibrium interface distance and check whether DFT reproduces the experimentally observed ~3.5 Å minimum. Method Three versions were computed. Because their structures differ, total energy cannot be compared directly between versions; only the energy trend within each version is compared, with d = 4.0 Å as the per-version reference. Version Functional Structure origin Interface arrangement 07-hetero vdW-DF2 vdW-DF2 relaxed V-Se₃-V (asymmetric) 08-hetero-v2 vdW-DF2 same as 07, flipped so Se faces Se Se₃-V-Se₃ (symmetric) 09-hetero-d2 PBE+D2 PBE+D2 relaxed Se₃-V-Se₃ (symmetric) 07 vs 08: same functional, same structure — interface arrangement only (V-facing vs Se-facing). Total energy is directly comparable. 07/08 vs 09: functional and relaxed structure both differ — total energy not directly comparable. Result All three decrease monotonically over 2.5–4.0 Å — no minimum within the scanned range. d (Å) 07 vdW-DF2 V-Se₃-V (eV) 08 vdW-DF2 Se₃-V-Se₃ (eV) 09 PBE+D2 Se₃-V-Se₃ (eV) 2.5 −3.73 −6.21 −8.31 3.0 −2.00 −4.10 −4.89 3.5 −0.84 −1.61 −1.84 4.0 0.00 0.00 0.00 Energies relative to d = 4.0 Å within each version (data: data/hetero-energy-vs-sep.csv , with absolute E KS ). Fig. 1. Relative energy vs interface separation for the three versions (each referenced to its own d = 4.0 Å). Fig. 2. Side views at d = 3.5 Å: 07 (vdW-DF2, V-Se₃-V asymmetric), 08 (vdW-DF2, Se₃-V-Se₃ symmetric), 09 (PBE+D2, Se₃-V-Se₃ symmetric). Discussion All three keep decreasing at d = 2.5 Å — no minimum is found within 2.5–4.0 Å. 07 vs 08 (same functional, interface only): the Se–Se symmetric arrangement (08) has a steeper slope than V-facing (07), and its total energy at d = 4.0 Å is 0.60 eV lower — i.e. the Se–Se interface binds more strongly. 09 (PBE+D2) cannot be compared directly to 07/08 because both functional and relaxed structure differ. This monotonic behavior motivated the dangling-bond hypothesis and the H-termination test (see H termination ), where passivating the VSe₃ interface Se restores a minimum near d ≈ 3.5 Å."}, {"p": "iterations/iter001-vesper-original/hf2se9-stability/index.html", "t": "Hf₂Se₉ relaxation stability: functional & vdW scan", "x": "Hf₂Se₉ relaxation stability: functional & vdW scan Source: reports/hf2se9-stability/ (+ docs/), reports/initial-structures/failure-analysis.md, meetings 2026-03-19/04-07. Goal Find a DFT setting under which Hf₂Se₉ molecule relaxation converges stably and reproduces the experimental structure. Prior calculations repeatedly failed (cell expansion 16–53%, structure collapse). Four axes are examined independently to isolate the cause: exchange–correlation functional, basis set, DFT+U, and CNT confinement. The test system is the Hf₂Se₉ molecule (11 atoms, 25 Å cubic cell, Γ-only) — the lightest case, used to scan settings quickly. Pass/fail criteria (TEM reference Hf–Hf = 3.6 Å) Criterion Success Failure Hf–Hf distance 3.5–3.8 Å held < 3.0 Å (collapse) or > 5.0 Å (decomposition) Force convergence < 0.01 eV/Å not converged (> 1000 steps) Structure confacial bioctahedron held Se₃ triangle distorted, Hf escapes Experimental agreement Hf–Hf deviation < 5% deviation > 10% Method — Phase A: exchange-correlation + vdW scan Fixed: basis EnergyShift 0.01 Ry (136 meV), U = 0, no CNT. Variable: functional + vdW method. # Functional vdW Code Purpose A1 PBE none SIESTA baseline — does structure hold without vdW? A2 PBE + Grimme D2 fdf2grimme SIESTA reproduce previous setup A3 vdW-DF2 (LMKLL) built-in SIESTA revPBE exchange, non-local correlation A4 PBE + D3(BJ) DFT-D3 SIESTA coordination-number-dependent C₆ A5 PBE + D3(BJ) IVDW=12 VASP plane-wave reference — isolate NAO-basis issue A6 HSE06 + D3(BJ) IVDW=12 VASP hybrid functional — exchange accuracy Result Phase A — Hf–Hf distances Test Functional Basis Code Hf–Hf (Å) Δ(3.6 Å) Converged Note A1 PBE 136 meV SIESTA 3.857 +7% ✅ 14 steps holds even without vdW A2 PBE+D2 136 meV SIESTA — — ❌ SCF not converged (3 failures, cause not identified) A3 vdW-DF2 136 meV SIESTA 3.967 +10% ✅ 13 steps revPBE exchange A4 PBE+D3(BJ) 136 meV SIESTA 3.857 +7% ✅ 14 steps = A1 (D3 has no effect on an isolated molecule) A5 PBE+D3 PAW VASP 2.934 −18.5% ✅ plane-wave reference A6 HSE06+D3 PAW VASP 3.330 −7.5% ✅ closest overall Within SIESTA, PBE and PBE+D3(BJ) (3.857 Å) are closest to experiment; vdW-DF2 over-expands further (3.967 Å). VASP HSE06+D3 (3.330 Å) is closest of all, but the SIESTA↔VASP discrepancy is large. A2 (PBE+D2) repeatedly fails SCF convergence — cause not yet isolated. Fig. 1. Relaxed Hf₂Se₉ molecule: A1 (SIESTA PBE), A3 (SIESTA vdW-DF2), A5 (VASP PBE+D3), A6 (VASP HSE06+D3). Molecule vs chain (vdW-DF2) Molecule (05) Chain (06) Initial Hf–Hf 3.62 Å 3.62 Å Relaxed Hf–Hf 3.967 Å (+10%) 4.164 Å (+15.7%) vdW gap (chain) — 5.89 Å (+68%) Force 0.027 eV/Å (≈ converged) 0.39 eV/Å (unstable) Both over-expand relative to experiment (3.6 Å); the chain is worse because periodic vdW interactions accumulate and revPBE's extra repulsion accelerates the expansion. A separate PBE+D2 chain run reached 197 steps before OOM (Hf–Hf = 4.47 Å, max force = 0.094 eV/Å, not converged). Fig. 2. vdW-DF2 relaxed Hf₂Se₉ molecule (left) and chain (right). Why prior relaxations failed (archive analysis) Six standalone Hf₂Se₉-chain relaxation attempts in the archive: Attempt Condition Result 001_rlx neutral, D2, EnergyShift 10 meV c-axis +16% expansion 002_totcharge-2 charge −2 still expanding 003_originstruct 1-unit (11 atoms) c-axis −22% contraction (opposite direction) 005_basis50 EnergyShift 50 meV nearly unchanged (convergence unclear) 006_basis50_tot-2 50 meV + charge −2 c-axis +53% → chain decomposes Identified root causes: Grimme D2 Hf C₆ inaccuracy. C₆ sets the vdW attraction strength ($E_\\mathrm{disp} = -C_6/R^6$). D2 uses a fixed free-atom value; free Hf has C₆ = 1089.41 eV·Å⁶, but inside Hf₂Se₉ the Hf is surrounded by 6 Se, so its real C₆ should be much smaller. D2 ignores this environment dependence. D3(BJ) interpolates C₆ from the coordination number, fixing it; vdW-DF2 avoids empirical parameters entirely (vdW energy from the electron density). Basis-set sensitivity (BSSE). Large basis (10 meV) → 16% expansion; small basis (50 meV) → nearly unchanged. The vdW-gap energy (~tens of meV) is within the BSSE error. MD.VariableCell = T. Allowing c-axis relaxation lets the flat PES collapse or expand the vdW gap under tiny forces. Starting-structure dependence. The 2-unit-cell start expands; the 1-unit-cell bulk-extracted start contracts — opposite directions for the same system. Heterostructure \"false convergence.\" In the 3uc heterojunction, 1-step convergence is a VSe₃ cage effect (kinetic trapping), not a true minimum. By contrast VASP MD with DFT-D3 held Hf–Hf = 3.82 Å. vdW-correction comparison: D2 vs D3(BJ) vs vdW-DF2 Grimme D2 (Grimme 2006) adds dispersion on top of PBE: $$E_\\mathrm{disp} = -s_6 \\sum_{i with element-fixed C₆ (no environment dependence) and PBE exchange (s₆ = 0.75 for PBE). D3(BJ) (Grimme 2010) interpolates C₆ from the coordination number and adds a C₈ term with Becke–Johnson damping. vdW-DF2 (Lee 2010) is non-empirical: $$E_\\mathrm{vdW\\text{-}DF2} = E_x^{\\mathrm{revPBE}} + E_c^{\\mathrm{LDA}} + E_c^{\\mathrm{nl}},\\qquad E_c^{\\mathrm{nl}} = \\tfrac12 \\iint n(\\mathbf{r})\\,\\Phi(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{r}')\\,n(\\mathbf{r}')\\,d\\mathbf{r}\\,d\\mathbf{r}'$$ where the non-local correlation $E_c^{\\mathrm{nl}}$ is computed directly from the electron density $n(\\mathbf{r})$ with kernel $\\Phi$, and uses revPBE exchange (more repulsive than PBE). Symbols: $C_6^{ij}$ = pairwise dispersion coefficient, $r_{ij}$ = interatomic distance, $f_\\mathrm{damp}$ = short-range damping, $s_6$ = global scaling. Feature D2 D3(BJ) vdW-DF2 C₆ determination fixed (1 per element) CN-dependent interpolation direct from density Environment adaptivity none via CN fully self-consistent Exchange functional PBE PBE revPBE damping zero (discontinuous) BJ (smooth) not needed (seamless) SIESTA support built-in (grimme.fdf) conditional (libdftd3; not in 5.2.x) built-in (XC.functional VDW) Reliability for Hf₂Se₉ low (failures observed) high (VASP-validated) high (theoretical) Comparison methodology (how \"better\" is judged) Total energy cannot be compared across functionals — each approximates $E_{xc}$ differently and uses a different energy reference (e.g. vdW-DF2 uses revPBE, D2 uses PBE). The valid comparisons are: (1) relaxed structure parameters vs experiment (most important — Hf–Hf vs TEM 3.6 Å); (2) relative energies within the same functional (e.g. the 41 meV/atom TP–TAP split in vdW-DF2); (3) binding/cohesive energy within a functional; and (4) convergence and structural stability. Phase C — CNT confinement (plan) Experimentally Hf₂Se₉ is synthesized/observed inside a CNT. The Phase C question is whether the CNT actually stabilizes the geometry toward the experimental Hf–Hf, or merely traps a metastable geometry by preventing motion. The first run is planned as \"CNT atoms fixed, Hf₂Se₉ relaxed only\", following the JACS 2021 NbTe₃/VTe₃/TiTe₃@CNT protocol (10.1021/jacs.0c10175). A diameter scan over CNT chiralities (≈ 12 Å inner diameter needed; (9,9) and (16,0) as starting candidates) precedes relaxation and MD. Status: structure scaffolds only; a (9,9)+Hf₂Se₉ assembly was generated. Fig. 3. Hf₂Se₉ in a (9,9) CNT, side view (assembled structure; calculation not yet run). Discussion / next steps Confirm optimal XC + EnergyShift (Phase A/B), then DFT+U scan (Phase D), then CNT (Phase C). Resolve A2 (PBE+D2) SCF failure; obtain QE A7 to separate code dependence. Validate the chosen functional on the chain, since D3 has no effect on an isolated molecule."}, {"p": "iterations/iter001-vesper-original/index.html", "t": "VSe₃ TP/TAP structure & Hf-doped molecule junction in CNTs active", "x": "VSe₃ TP/TAP structure & Hf-doped molecule junction in CNTs active Members: yw-choi, bychanlee, nangman98 · created 2026-03-16 Idea Transition-metal trichalcogenides (MX₃) form 1D chains in either a TP (trigonal prismatic) or TAP (trigonal anti-prismatic) coordination. Te-based MX₃ are reported as TAP and Nb as TP, but VSe₃ had no experimental report . The goal is to identify the structure of a VSe₃ single chain synthesized inside a carbon nanotube (CNT) by atomic-resolution STEM — confirming the TP structure for the first time — and to compare TP vs TAP stability with DFT. Core questions (as stated in the README) For a VSe₃ single chain, which is more stable, TP or TAP? How does TP/TAP preference differ between Se and Te (referencing existing JACS Te data)? Is an Hf-doped molecule structure stabilized inside a CNT? Extension Hf doping → observation of a 1D molecule junction + structure proposal. Ultimate target: a 1D metal superlattice structure (concept proposal). Method / scope DFT with SIESTA (NAO basis), VASP (PAW), and Quantum ESPRESSO, comparing exchange–correlation functionals (PBE, vdW-DF2, PBE+D2, PBE+D3(BJ), HSE06) and van der Waals corrections. The work spans VSe₃ TP/TAP chains, the Hf₂Se₉ molecule/chain, the VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉ heterostructure, and (planned) CNT-confined and transport calculations. Planned figures (draft, from README) Figure Content Fig 1 VSe₃ inserted in CNT; TP structure confirmed by STEM Fig 2 DFT: TP vs TAP stability, band structure, CNT effect Fig 3 (extension) Hf doping → 1D molecule junction + structure proposal Pages See Status for the current state, and the result pages below for each completed analysis. Key results so far: TP is more stable than TAP for VSe₃ ; the isolated Hf₂Se₉ molecule/chain relaxes to an over-expanded Hf–Hf distance; the bare VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉ separation curve has no minimum, but H termination of the VSe₃ interface Se restores a minimum near d ≈ 3.5 Å ."}, {"p": "iterations/iter001-vesper-original/previous-calculations/index.html", "t": "Previous calculations review (archive)", "x": "Previous calculations review (archive) Source: reports/previous-calculations/previous-calculations.md (dated 2026-03-16). Goal Summarize the ~1-year-old VSe₃/Hf₂Se₉ calculations preserved in two archive directories ( 101_VSe3-Hf2Se9/ , VSe3-Hf2Se9/ ) to identify what is reusable for the current project. Across VASP (MD, hybrid DFT), SIESTA (band/PDOS, transport), and Quantum ESPRESSO (functional comparison), the prior work covered Hf₂Se₉ molecule/chain stability, electronic structure, optical properties, and transport — but the core goal, a 1D molecule junction, was left incomplete because the Hf-doped molecule structure was unstable . Method (archive settings) Code Use Key settings VASP MD, relaxation, hybrid functional ENCUT 400–500 eV, EDIFF 1e-6–1e-8, DFT-D3(BJ) SIESTA band, PDOS, transport (TranSIESTA) MeshCutoff 500 Ry, PBE+Grimme vdW Quantum ESPRESSO functional comparison ecutwfc 100 Ry, B3LYP/PBE/LDA Common parameters: molecule/chain in a 25 Å cubic cell (isolated); heterojunction 25 × 25 × 74.7 Å; k-points Γ-only (molecule), 1×1×4 (chain/transport); vdW IVDW=10 (DFT-D3(BJ), VASP) / Grimme (SIESTA); MD Langevin thermostat 300 K, timestep 0.5–2.0 fs. Result — what was computed Hf₂Se₉ single molecule: VASP MD (NVT 300 K, up to 500 steps) confirmed molecular stability; SIESTA PBE band + PDOS near E F . Hf₂Se₉ chain: VASP MD (up to 1000 steps), SIESTA PBE+Grimme band + PDOS. VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉ heterostructure (V₂₀Se₇₂Hf₂, 94 atoms): VASP MD, SIESTA band/PDOS, Hubbard-U test, CNT+Hf₂Se₉ structure, Mulliken charge analysis. Transport: TranSIESTA + TBtrans I-V curve over 7 voltage points (0.00–0.30 V) for a 20-unit-cell Hf₂Se₉ molecular junction (k = 1×1×4, electronic temperature 400 K). Optical (hybrid): B3LYP (VASP) neutral and e-doped completed; HSE06 and PBE/LDA optical comparisons left incomplete (empty directories). Code comparison: SIESTA (LDA/PBE) vs QE (LDA/PBE/B3LYP) for the same structure. Auxiliary VSe3-Hf2Se9/ : single chains, molecule, heterojunction (separation + angle tests), Hf-doped VSe₃ chain, NbSe₃ comparison system, and a 3-system transport set (Hf₂Se₉ / Hf-doped / VSe₃). Mulliken charge analysis of the heterojunction: Hf atoms gain electrons (Hf1 −0.27e, Hf2 −0.15e); interface V atoms hold ~0.2e more than bulk V — significant interfacial charge transfer. Discussion — incomplete / failed Hf-doped molecule structure: unstable, did not converge → to be retried with CNT-confined MD. HSE06 calculations: directories created but never run. PBE/LDA optical comparison: not done. VSe₃ TP vs TAP: not done at the time → the core task of the current project. Reusable for the current project Archive data Reuse VSe₃ chain relaxation/band TP reference point, initial-structure reference Hf-doped chain reference for molecule-junction recalculation NbSe₃ comparison protocol reference for the Se-vs-Te study Transport protocol reuse input files for future transport calculations DFT+U test (U = 1,3,7 eV) reference for U-parameter selection"}, {"p": "iterations/iter001-vesper-original/status/index.html", "t": "Status", "x": "Status Synthesized from the meeting notes (latest 2026-04-16) and report tables. No standalone STATUS.md / comchart.yaml exists in the repo. Work-package status (as of 2026-04-07 meeting) Work package Status Comment VSe₃ TP/TAP comparison Done TP consistently more stable Hf₂Se₉ mol/chain (vdW-DF2) Done Over-expansion confirmed Phase A functional scan In progress A2-v3, A7 (QE) remaining Phase B basis scan In progress B1 at step 67 Hetero separation test Done All 3 versions monotonically decreasing Chain D2 In progress OOM, resubmission needed CNT stabilization study Preparation Diameter scan needed Phase D (DFT+U) Not started — Latest progress (2026-04-16 meeting) H termination of the VSe₃ interface Se restores a separation-energy minimum. With H, the bond/gap/tilt H-orientation modes were compared (vdW-DF2 single-point, 44 atoms, Se–H = 1.46 Å). The gap mode is most stable over the whole range; its minimum sits at d = 3.5–4.0 Å (the two points differ by ~4 meV). bond is 1.11 eV higher and tilt 3.72 eV higher than gap . See H termination . VSe₃ TAP relaxed structure delivered to the experimental team (Dr. Yangjin Lee), for STEM image simulation. vdW-DF2 relaxed: c = 5.535 Å, V–Se = 2.508 Å, V–V = 2.768 Å. Heterostructure relax → transport setup: action item open (not yet done). Hf₂Se₉ molecule Phase A scan — result snapshot Reference experimental Hf–Hf distance (TEM): 3.6 Å. (Source: 2026-04-07 meeting, stability report.) Test Method Code Hf–Hf (Å) Δ(3.6 Å) Status A1 PBE SIESTA 3.857 +7% ✅ 14 steps A2 PBE+D2 SIESTA — — ❌ SCF not converged A3 vdW-DF2 (LMKLL) SIESTA 3.967 +10% ✅ 13 steps A4 PBE+D3(BJ) SIESTA 3.857 +7% ✅ 14 steps (= A1) A5 PBE+D3 VASP 2.934 −18.5% ✅ A6 HSE06+D3 VASP 3.330 −7.5% ✅ A7 PBE QE — — 📋 prepared Within SIESTA, PBE and PBE+D3(BJ) give the closest value to experiment (3.857 Å); VASP HSE06+D3 is closest overall (3.330 Å) but the SIESTA–VASP gap is large. Detail: Hf₂Se₉ stability . Open action items (2026-04-16) Adopt gap mode, d = 3.5 Å as the relaxation starting structure (under discussion). Set up transport calculation once the heterostructure relax completes (open)."}, {"p": "iterations/iter001-vesper-original/vse3-tp-tap/index.html", "t": "VSe₃ TP vs TAP stability", "x": "VSe₃ TP vs TAP stability Source: meetings 2026-03-19, 2026-04-07, 2026-04-16; initial-structures report. Goal Determine which 1D chain coordination — TP (trigonal prismatic) or TAP (trigonal anti-prismatic) — is more stable for VSe₃, supporting the experimental (STEM) claim that the CNT-confined VSe₃ single chain is TP. The two differ by a 60° rotation between adjacent Se₃ triangles along the chain axis: in TP the triangles are aligned (top view shows 3 Se), in TAP adjacent triangles are rotated 60° (top view shows a 6-pointed star). Method Initial structures were built from literature parameters with build_vse3.py : TP from d VSe = 2.45 Å, d VV = 3.09 Å (covalent-radii sum, Cordero 2008 + NbSe₃ scaling, Hodeau 1978); TAP is the same with a 60° rotation (c = 6.18 Å, 8 atoms). Both were relaxed in SIESTA. TP/TAP stability was then compared with two functionals, vdW-DF2 (LMKLL) and PBE+D2. Within a single functional the total-energy difference is a valid comparison; across functionals it is not (see stability methodology ). Result TP is more stable than TAP in both functionals. Functional ΔE (TP relative to TAP) TP band character vdW-DF2 TP more stable by 41 meV/atom metallic PBE+D2 TP more stable by 20 meV/atom metallic The TP band structure is metallic in both methods. (Source: 2026-04-07 meeting; the 41 meV/atom vdW-DF2 value is also quoted in the stability-comparison-method note.) Fig. 1. vdW-DF2 relaxed VSe₃ TP (left) and TAP (right) chains. Fig. 2. Top views (initial structures): TP — aligned Se₃ triangles (3 Se visible); TAP — adjacent triangles rotated 60° (6-pointed star). Fig. 3. VSe₃ TP band structure: vdW-DF2 (left) and PBE+D2 (right). Metallic in both. Discussion The TAP relaxed structure was delivered to the experimental team (Dr. Yangjin Lee) for STEM image simulation (2026-04-16). Its vdW-DF2 relaxed local parameters: c = 5.535 Å, V–Se = 2.508 Å, V–V = 2.768 Å. A planned extension is a Se-vs-Te (VSe₃ vs VTe₃) TP/TAP comparison; VTe₃ TP/TAP initial structures were built (cases 03/04) but those relaxations are listed as 2nd priority and not reported here."}, {"p": "log/index.html", "t": "Log", "x": "Log 작업 일지 + 미팅 노트 (날짜순, 최신 위) 2026-07-02 — Since the 06-19 meeting: 9-cell convergence → DFT+Σ applied → 3-way + corrected I–V 1 · Scattering-region length convergence 9 VSe₃ unit cells per electrode: $T(E_F)$ = 0.348 ≈ 0.354 (3 cells), single channel $\\tau_0$ = 0.989 — geometry, molecular PDOS, transmission all stable by 7–9 cells. 2 · DFT+Σ molecular-level correction Inputs: $\\Delta_i=\\varepsilon_i^\\text{B3LYP}-\\varepsilon_i^\\text{vdW-DF2}$ (vacuum-aligned): $\\Delta_\\text{HOMO}$ −1.01 / $\\Delta_\\text{LUMO}$ −0.29 eV; gap 0.02 → 0.75 eV. Cross-check: same-code VASP PBE→B3LYP gives a ≈0.42 eV smaller (near-rigid) shift — code offset can't be separated → baseline = vdW-DF2 , applied as a relative scissor. Projector: isolated-molecule MOs, not MPSH (Hf₂Se₉ is covalently fused — the bare device block has no clean levels near $E_F$). $\\Delta H = S\\,C\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta_i)\\,C^\\dagger S$, level-1 TBT.dH . Applied (9-cell): $T(E_F)$ 0.355 → 0.051 , molecular PDOS$(E_F)$ 20 → 1.2 /eV; channel moves to the HOMO resonance (−0.095 eV, $\\tau_0$ 0.373; at $E_F$ 0.989 → 0.124). Gap check: isolated gap opens 0.023 → 0.751 eV; device frontier spacing 0.180 eV (lead hybridization, $E_F$ pinning) — consistent with the DFT+Σ literature. 3 · Three-way transmission + corrected I–V Disconnected done: $T(E_F)$ = 0.000 → pristine 0.70 / hetero 0.348 / disconnected 0 — the molecule is the sole conduction bridge. Corrected Landauer I–V (zero-bias $T(E)$): ±0.5 V +5.04/−2.70 → +4.30/−1.04 μA; rectification $|I_+/I_-|$ 1.9 → 4.1. Posted. Level correction Section C (corrected $T(E)$, PDOS before/after + zoom, eigenchannel isosurfaces, I–V — Fig. 6–11); transmission Fig. 2 (3-way overlay). Next. True finite-bias chain (0 → ±0.5 V, corrected $\\Delta H$); charge-transfer $\\Delta\\rho$. → Results: level correction · Results: transmission · Methods: DFT+Σ implementation 2026-06-19 — Convergence checks done; ΔH correction derived Result. Convergence checks complete; B3LYP molecular gap 0.752 eV (vacuum-aligned), vs vdW-DF2 0.024 eV. Decided. The ΔH level correction was derived on the board; implement it via the TBtrans δH mechanism (no source patch) and document the workflow. Re-relaxation at 9 VSe₃ unit cells per electrode assigned. → Methods: DFT+Σ implementation · Results: level correction 2026-06-12 — Group meeting: structure, gap, passivation, rotation, transport Presented. VSe₃ TP > TAP by 0.163 eV/formula (metallic); Hf₂Se₉ gap 0.024 eV; H-passivation 3 configs, min $d$ ≈ 3.5 Å (top most stable); rotation minimum at 60°/180° (60° staggered, +43.3 meV); pristine $T(E)$ length-dependence = η broadening artifact (ballistic at η = 0.001 eV); heterojunction $T(E_F)$ ≈ 1.6 → 0.35, eigenchannel runs through the molecule. Decided. One week of convergence checks (scattering-region length, device k-grid and η, benchmarks, disconnected 3-way comparison) → then B3LYP molecular-orbital correction (DFT+Σ), charge transfer, I–V. → Results: junction · Results: transmission · Results: H termination 2026-06-02 — η-broadening artifact resolved Result. The apparent length dependence of pristine $T(E)$ is an η-broadening artifact, not physics — lowering the contour broadening η 0.05 → 0.001 eV restores length independence. Production transport settles at η = 0.005 eV . → Results: transmission 2026-05-29 — Heterojunction transport discussion Decided. Pristine-VSe₃ transport check; settle the interfacial structure first; split the molecular PDOS into Hf–Se / V–Se contributions. (Meeting has a single-file HTML replay.) 2026-05-13 — Group meeting: relaxation → transport handoff Result. Single-point scan keeps gap , d = 3.5 Å as the most stable H-termination; final call deferred to the relaxation. Decided. Once the relaxation converges, go straight to transport. The transport cell length is set by the perturbation range $\\Delta V(z)$ between the VSe₃+molecule and VSe₃-only SCF potentials. Judgment principle: physical sense first, numbers second. 2026-04-16 — Group meeting: H termination fixes the interface Result. Three H-orientation modes scanned at the VSe₃ interface (vdW-DF2, 44 atoms, Se–H = 1.46 Å): gap most stable with a separation minimum at d = 3.5–4.0 Å ( bond +1.11 eV, tilt +3.72 eV) — H termination restores the missing separation-energy minimum. Delivered. Relaxed VSe₃ TAP structure to the experimental team for STEM simulation: c = 5.535 Å, V–Se = 2.508 Å, V–V = 2.768 Å. Decided. gap , d = 3.5 Å becomes the relaxation starting structure; transport cell length to be set from the $\\Delta V(z)$ perturbation range. → Results: H termination 2026-04-07 — Group meeting: structure & stability round-up Presented. VSe₃ TP consistently more stable than TAP; Hf₂Se₉ functional scan — PBE+D2 fails to converge the SCF while vdW-DF2 relaxes cleanly (Hf–Hf 3.967 Å vs TEM 3.6 Å); hetero separation-energy curves monotonic (no minimum without H termination). Decided. H termination of the interface is the top priority; the CNT-encapsulation study is shelved; PBE+D2 SCF debugging is dropped. → 01. TP vs TAP · 02. Hf₂Se₉ stability · 03. Hetero separation 2026-03-23 — Correspondence with the experimental team Exchanged. Structure information with the experimental collaborators (STEM side) — inputs for the model building and the later STEM-simulation hand-off. 2026-03-19 — Initial structures built Result. Initial TP/TAP chain structures constructed from literature parameters ( build_vse3.py ; e.g. TP: d(V–Se) = 2.45 Å, d(V–V) = 3.09 Å from covalent radii + NbSe₃ scaling) — relaxation queued. 2026-03-16 — Project kickoff Background. MX₃ chains take TP or TAP coordination; Te members are reported TAP, NbSe₃ TP — VSe₃ had no experimental report until its synthesis inside a CNT (collaboration). Project goal: first-principles transport of the VSe₃/Hf₂Se₉ 1D heterojunction ( Motivation ). Full meeting notes + figures live in meetings/<YYYY-MM-DD>/ in the repository; the recent ones also have a single-file HTML meeting replay."}, {"p": "methods/dft-sigma/index.html", "t": "DFT+Σ via TBtrans δH — implementation", "x": "DFT+Σ via TBtrans δH — implementation 2026-06-19 · method · How the molecular-level correction enters the transport calculation, and why no SIESTA source patch is needed Summary The B3LYP→vdW-DF2 level correction is a DFT+Σ scissor shift. It does not require editing SIESTA/TBtrans source — TBtrans has a built-in δH (delta-H) mechanism that adds a correction matrix to the input Hamiltonian at read time. We build $\\Delta H$ with sisl, write it to a .dH.nc file, and point TBtrans at it with TBT.dH . Symbols & terms Symbol / term Meaning PAO $\\chi_\\mu$ Pseudo-atomic orbital — SIESTA's numerical atomic-orbital basis (each atom's s/p/d functions). Non-orthogonal : $\\langle\\chi_\\mu|\\chi_\\nu\\rangle=S_{\\mu\\nu}\\neq\\delta_{\\mu\\nu}$. $H$, $S$, $\\Delta H$ are all matrices in this basis. MO $\\phi_i$ Molecular orbital — an eigenstate of the isolated Hf₂Se₉ molecule, $H_{\\rm mol}c_i=\\varepsilon_i S_{\\rm mol}c_i$. Indexed relative to HOMO (HOMO, HOMO−1, LUMO, …). $C$ (MO coeff.) Expansion of each MO over PAOs, $|\\phi_i\\rangle=\\sum_\\mu C^i_\\mu|\\chi_\\mu\\rangle$ ($C^i_\\mu$ = weight of PAO $\\mu$ in MO $i$). Extracted from the molecule's WFSX. $S$ (overlap) PAO overlap $S_{\\mu\\nu}=\\langle\\chi_\\mu|\\chi_\\nu\\rangle$. Enters $\\Delta H$ on both sides because the basis is non-orthogonal. projector Operator $|\\phi_i\\rangle\\langle\\phi_i|$ that acts only on MO $i$. Adding $\\Delta_i$ times it shifts only that level. In PAO form: $\\Delta H=S\\,C\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta_i)\\,C^\\dagger S$. $\\Delta_i$ (scissor) Per-orbital energy shift $\\varepsilon_i^{\\rm B3LYP}-\\varepsilon_i^{\\rm vdW\\text{-}DF2}$ (vacuum-aligned) — the static self-energy $\\Sigma$ approximated as an energy-independent, orbital-diagonal shift. Question Answer Dedicated \"DFT+Σ\" command in SIESTA? No — implemented via the general δH mechanism (below). Patch the source? No. The δH file path is the supported hook. How? sisl builds $\\Delta H$ → deltaH.dH.nc → TBtrans applies $H'(k)=H(k)+\\delta H$. Standard name? DFT+Σ (DFT+Sigma) — molecular-junction level alignment. Why the correction is needed Semi-local functionals (PBE/vdW-DF2) carry a self-interaction error: occupied levels sit too high, virtual too low, so the HOMO–LUMO gap is underestimated (our molecule: vdW-DF2 0.02 eV vs B3LYP 0.75 eV). In off-resonant tunnelling the linear conductance follows a single-level Lorentzian $\\displaystyle T(E)=\\frac{\\Gamma_L\\,\\Gamma_R}{(E-\\varepsilon_0)^2+[(\\Gamma_L+\\Gamma_R)/2]^2},$ so the level offset $|\\varepsilon_0-E_F|$ governs $T(E_F)$. A gap squeezed onto $E_F$ inflates conductance. DFT+Σ shifts each molecular level by $\\Delta_i=\\varepsilon_i^{\\text{B3LYP}}-\\varepsilon_i^{\\text{vdW-DF2}}$ to restore the offset — the quasiparticle self-energy $\\Sigma$ approximated as a per-orbital constant (scissor). Because $\\Delta_i$ is energy-independent, the static self-energy equals a Hamiltonian correction: $\\displaystyle G(E)=\\big[(E+i\\eta)S-(H+\\Delta H)-\\Sigma_L-\\Sigma_R\\big]^{-1}, \\qquad \\Delta\\Sigma(E)=\\Delta H=\\text{const.}$ Hence TBtrans level-1 δH (no $E$/$k$ dependence) is exactly the right tool; for $T(E)$ the δH and δΣ paths are identical (they differ only in bond/orbital currents). Correction value — literature standard vs ours The shift magnitude $\\Delta_i$ can be defined two ways. The literature standard (Quek–Neaton; Fallaque 2022) shifts the occupied and virtual manifolds as blocks, using the ionization potential / electron affinity from ΔSCF ($\\mathrm{IP}=E(+e)-E(0)$, $\\mathrm{EA}=E(0)-E(-e)$): occupied levels shift by $-\\varepsilon_H-\\mathrm{IP}$ and virtual by $-\\varepsilon_L-\\mathrm{EA}$, plus a metal image-charge term. Our variant uses the per-orbital B3LYP−vdW-DF2 Kohn–Sham eigenvalue difference (vacuum-level aligned) and omits the image-charge term to first order. Literature standard Ours magnitude $-\\varepsilon_H-\\mathrm{IP}$ / $-\\varepsilon_L-\\mathrm{EA}$ (IP/EA = ΔSCF) $\\varepsilon_i(\\text{B3LYP})-\\varepsilon_i(\\text{vdW-DF2})$ (KS difference) granularity occupied / virtual block per-orbital $\\Delta_i$ image-charge included omitted (first-order upper bound) Using vdW-DF2 as the baseline keeps the correction consistent with the device functional. The caveat: B3LYP (VASP) and vdW-DF2 (SIESTA) are different codes, so a residual common offset remains. A same-code cross-check (VASP PBE single point on the vacuum molecule — same code and geometry as the B3LYP reference) isolates the pure exchange–correlation shift: $\\Delta_\\text{HOMO}$ = −0.58, $\\Delta_\\text{LUMO}$ = +0.17 eV (gap 0.001 → 0.751), which is ≈0.42 eV smaller (near-rigid) than the vdW-DF2-referenced $\\Delta_\\text{HOMO}$ = −1.01 eV. The offset mixes exchange–correlation and code differences and cannot be cleanly separated, so we keep the baseline at the device functional (vdW-DF2) and apply $\\Delta_i$ as a relative scissor — the rigid code offset then cancels. Workflow STEP 1 (VASP) vacuum molecule: PBE, B3LYP → per-orbital Δᵢ = ε_i(B3LYP) − ε_i(PBE) STEP 2 (SIESTA) vacuum molecule: same PAO basis → single-molecule MO coefficients STEP 3 (sisl) read device .TSHS → build ΔH (Hf₂Se₉ block) → write deltaH.dH.nc STEP 4 (TBtrans) fdf: TBT.dH deltaH.dH.nc → recompute T(E) STEP 5 compare T(E) / conductance before vs after Reference inputs — where each quantity comes from Quantity What Source $\\Delta_i$ per-orbital level shift 05-hf2se9-mol/mo_delta_orbital.csv $\\varepsilon_i$(B3LYP) vacuum-molecule B3LYP levels 05-hf2se9-mol/B3LYP/EIGENVAL (VASP) $\\varepsilon_i$(vdW-DF2) vacuum-molecule vdW-DF2 levels 05-hf2se9-mol/DF2/relax2/siesta.EIG (SIESTA) $C$ vacuum-molecule MO coefficients 05-hf2se9-mol/DF2/mo_coeff/extracted/ (from WFSX) $S$ device molecular-block overlap 11-hetero60-transport/N25_k100/0bias/trans.TSHS $\\Delta_i$ are vacuum-level aligned before differencing; the energy window keeps only orbitals within $E_F\\pm2$ eV (orbitals further out do not contribute to transport). When & how it enters the calculation Timing — post-SCF, non-self-consistent. The correction is applied after the device vdW-DF2 SCF has converged; the SCF is not re-run. Structure and bonding stay at their vdW-DF2 values — only the molecular level positions move. The flow: device DFT (TranSIESTA) → $H$, $S$ ( trans.TSHS ) add ΔH to the molecular block: $H\\to H+\\Delta H$ TBtrans Green's function with the corrected Hamiltonian: $G(E)=[(E+i\\eta)S-(H+\\Delta H)-\\Sigma_L-\\Sigma_R]^{-1}$ corrected transmission $T(E)=\\mathrm{Tr}[\\Gamma_L\\,G\\,\\Gamma_R\\,G^\\dagger]$ The ΔH matrix — step-by-step derivation (non-orthogonal basis) ① Correction operator. Shift each molecular orbital $\\phi_i$ by $\\Delta_i$ — a scissor projector. Only the level position moves; the internal hopping (wavefunction shape) and the electrode coupling $\\Gamma$ stay at their vdW-DF2 values: $\\displaystyle \\Delta\\hat H = \\sum_i \\Delta_i\\,|\\phi_i\\rangle\\langle\\phi_i|$ ② Expand the MO over atomic orbitals (PAO): $|\\phi_i\\rangle=\\sum_\\mu C^i_\\mu|\\chi_\\mu\\rangle$, where the $C^i_\\mu$ are the molecular-orbital coefficients (STEP 2) and $\\chi_\\mu$ the atomic orbitals. ③ Take matrix elements in the PAO basis: $\\displaystyle (\\Delta H)_{\\mu\\nu}=\\langle\\chi_\\mu|\\Delta\\hat H|\\chi_\\nu\\rangle =\\sum_i\\Delta_i\\,\\langle\\chi_\\mu|\\phi_i\\rangle\\,\\langle\\phi_i|\\chi_\\nu\\rangle.$ ④ Where the overlap $S$ enters — the step that is easy to drop. The PAOs are non-orthogonal, $\\langle\\chi_\\mu|\\chi_\\lambda\\rangle=S_{\\mu\\lambda}\\neq\\delta_{\\mu\\lambda}$, so $\\displaystyle \\langle\\chi_\\mu|\\phi_i\\rangle=\\sum_\\lambda C^i_\\lambda\\,S_{\\mu\\lambda}=(S\\,C^i)_\\mu.$ Substituting into ③ gives the working form: $\\displaystyle \\boxed{\\;\\Delta H = S\\,C\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta_i)\\,C^{\\dagger}\\,S\\;}$ In an orthogonal basis ($S=I$) this reduces to the textbook $\\sum_i\\Delta_i c_i c_i^\\dagger$ — the $S$ on both sides is precisely the non-orthogonal correction. ⑤ Verify the levels move by exactly $\\Delta_j$. With the generalized eigenproblem $Hc_j=\\varepsilon_j Sc_j$ and $S$-orthonormality $C^\\dagger SC=I$ (hence $C^\\dagger Sc_j=e_j$, the $j$-th unit vector): $\\displaystyle (H+\\Delta H)c_j=\\varepsilon_j Sc_j + S\\,C\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta)\\,e_j=(\\varepsilon_j+\\Delta_j)\\,Sc_j.$ The eigenvector $c_j$ is unchanged and the eigenvalue moves $\\varepsilon_j\\to\\varepsilon_j+\\Delta_j$ — exactly the intended shift. Adding to a single onsite element $H_{\\mu\\mu}$ alone does not achieve this in a non-orthogonal basis (verified in sisl #129), which is why $S$ must sit on both sides. This system needs the isolated-molecule MO — not MPSH. The usual robust route diagonalises the in-device molecular block (MPSH), which auto-normalises $C$. But here Hf₂Se₉ is covalently fused into the VSe₃ chain, so the bare molecular block has no clean molecular levels near $E_F$: the valence manifold sits ~6 eV too deep, there is an ~8 eV gap, and the $E_F\\pm2$ eV window is empty — the block's weight at $E_F$ is chain hybridization, not a molecular orbital. So we build the projector from the isolated (vacuum) molecule MO coefficients (the same molecule/geometry used for $\\Delta_i$) and register them into the device basis, rescaling to $C^\\dagger S_\\text{dev} C = I$: import sisl, numpy as np H = sisl.get_sile('device.TSHS').read_hamiltonian() S = H.Sk(format='array').real # device overlap mol = np.load('mol_orbital_idx.npy') # Hf2Se9 PAO indices in the device SM = S[np.ix_(mol, mol)] # MO coefficients from the ISOLATED vacuum-molecule SIESTA run (STEP 2) — NOT an # MPSH diagonalization of the in-device block (no clean molecular levels there) C = np.load('mo_coeff_isolated.npy') # (n_mol_orb, n_MO), same geometry as Δ_i delta = np.load('delta_i.npy') # eV, per orbital, |ε − E_F| ≲ 2 eV window # register into the device basis: rescale so Cᵀ S_dev C = I norm = np.einsum('mi,mn,ni->i', C.conj(), SM, C).real C = C / np.sqrt(norm) dH_block = SM @ C @ np.diag(delta) @ C.conj().T @ SM # S C diag(Δ) Cᵀ S dH = H.copy(); dH.empty(); dH.set_nsc([1,1,1]) # molecule closed in unit cell for a, mu in enumerate(mol): for b, nu in enumerate(mol): dH[mu, nu] = dH_block[a, b] sisl.get_sile('deltaH.dH.nc', 'w').write_delta(dH) # no k=/E= → level-1 The isolated-molecule build reproduces the earlier attempt to ~10 meV. (For the 9-cell run the projector should come from a molecular single point at the device geometry, with isolated↔device orientation registration.) TBtrans input TBT.dH deltaH.dH.nc Verify TBtrans stdout logs that it read the δH file. For $T(E)$ comparison, TBT.dH and TBT.dSE give the same result. Pitfalls Supercell (non-square submatrix). A .TSHS packs supercell couplings into one matrix, so slicing the molecule block can give a non-square array. Close the molecule inside the unit cell with H.set_nsc([1,1,1]) — the manual states Bloch \"phases are only added on super-cell elements, not unit-cell elements\", so a unit-cell block needs no phase correction. Never diagonalise the 27 supercell blocks separately. Non-orthogonal ΔH ($S$ on both sides). Adding a bare onsite term ( H[i,i] += … ) does not move the generalized eigenvalue ($H\\psi=\\varepsilon S\\psi$) as intended (sisl #129). Use $\\Delta H = S\\,C\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta_i)\\,C^\\dagger S$; the orthogonal limit $S=I$ recovers the textbook form. Post-check (gate before recomputing T). After applying ΔH, confirm the molecular PDOS / MPSH peaks actually moved by $\\Delta_i$. A mismatch means a missing $S$, bad normalization, or an index-mapping error. Manual / source basis (verbatim) These are documented features, not guesses — read directly from the manuals/source on 2026-06-19: Item Verbatim Source write_delta level no k / E arg ⇒ \"level-1: no E or k dependence\" sisl delta source correction \"$H'(k)=H(k)+\\delta H(E,k)+\\delta\\Sigma(E,k)$\" sisl TBT.dH \"terms defined in this file are added to the Hamiltonian when calculating the orbital currents\" TBtrans 5.2 Guide TBT.dSE \"δΣ does not enter the calculation of the bond-currents, whereas δH does\" TBtrans 5.2 Guide supercell phase \"phases are only added on super cell elements, not unit cell elements\" TBtrans 5.2 Guide How δH actually enters — per (E,k), verified from source The table above is the what ; this is the how , read from the sisl delta source and the TBtrans reference (2026-07-01) so the level-1 choice is not taken on faith. The file carries a \"level\" tag. sisl write_delta tags the matrix by which arguments are present ( _get_lvl_k_E ): level-1 = no E or k dependence , level-2 = k, level-3 = E, level-4 = both. We call write_delta(dH) with no k= / E= → level-1, one constant matrix . That is exactly the static-self-energy scissor $\\Delta\\Sigma(E)=\\Delta H=\\text{const}$ — choosing level-1 is the scissor approximation. It enters inside the Green's-function inverse, at every (E,k). sisl's class docstring gives $H'(k)=H(k)+\\delta H(E,k)+\\delta\\Sigma(E,k)$; TBtrans forms $G(E,k)=\\big[(E+i\\eta)S(k)-H(k)-\\delta H-\\Sigma_L(E,k)-\\Sigma_R(E,k)\\big]^{-1}$ at each energy / k-point. Because our term is level-1, the same $\\delta H$ is added at every (E,k) (a level-4 term would take precedence where its grid matches). δH vs δΣ: identical in $G$, $T(E)$, and DOS; they differ only in bond/orbital currents (δΣ excluded). We use δH so orbital currents stay physical. Why set_nsc([1,1,1]) : TBtrans \"add[s] the phases on all elements of δ\" but only on super-cell elements. Closing the molecule block inside the unit cell leaves no super-cell element, so no Bloch phase $e^{ik\\cdot R}$ is wrongly applied to $\\delta H$ (otherwise one must pre-multiply δ by the negative phase). Verified 2026-07-01: sisl delta source · SIESTA 5.2 TBtrans reference . Where the method comes from — literature precedent Two papers are the direct precedent for STEP 3 (adding Σ to the molecular Hamiltonian via the MPSH block); both correct DFT-overestimated molecular-junction conductance to experimental levels: Montes & Vázquez, Appl. Sci. 11, 802 (2021) — the original SIESTA/TRANSIESTA implementation: the self-energy correction is added explicitly to the system Hamiltonian as an orbital-dependent operator. Benzenediamine/Au; DFT overestimates conductance 5–13×, DFT+Σ brings it to 1.0–1.8× of experiment. Fallaque et al., JPCM 34, 435901 (2022) — an efficient TRANSIESTA re-implementation with an improved (infinite-reflection) image charge, benchmarked on acene/Au with excellent agreement. Its procedure — extract $H_\\text{mol}$, $S_\\text{mol}$ from .TSHS (sisl + NumPy + ASE) → MPSH diagonalise $H_\\text{mol}\\psi=\\varepsilon S_\\text{mol}\\psi$ → add Σ → replace — is the same scheme as ours, with one difference: for our covalently-fused molecule the in-device MPSH block has no clean molecular levels near $E_F$, so we build the projector from the isolated molecule instead (see the derivation above). How ours differs (stated honestly): both precedents are organic molecule + Au ; no direct precedent for an inorganic 1D chain (VSe₃) electrode was found. Our shift is the B3LYP−vdW-DF2 KS difference rather than the standard ΔSCF IP/EA, and we omit the image-charge term that both papers include. References Fallaque et al. , J. Phys. Condens. Matter 34 , 435901 (2022) — TRANSIESTA DFT+Σ + image charge, acene/Au (full text verified). DOI 10.1088/1361-648X/ac89c4 Montes & Vázquez , Appl. Sci. 11 , 802 (2021) — original SIESTA/TRANSIESTA orbital-dependent operator (full text not accessed; via Fallaque citation). DOI 10.3390/app11020802 Neaton, Hybertsen & Louie , Phys. Rev. Lett. 97 , 216405 (2006) — origin of the correction. GW shows the metal-surface image charge renormalizes (narrows) the molecular HOMO–LUMO gap relative to gas phase — an interface polarization effect DFT misses. This is the physical basis for adding a self-energy $\\Sigma$ to the molecular levels. DOI 10.1103/PhysRevLett.97.216405 Quek, Venkataraman, Choi, Louie, Hybertsen & Neaton , Nano Lett. 7 , 3477 (2007) — the DFT+Σ transport recipe. Applies the gas-phase gap correction plus an image-charge scissor to benzenediamine/Au, aligning the molecular levels so the computed conductance matches experiment (bare DFT overestimates it). The direct template for our per-orbital scissor. DOI 10.1021/nl072058i Strange, Rostgaard, Häkkinen & Thygesen , Phys. Rev. B 83 , 115108 (2011) — validation against full GW. Self-consistent GW transport (no scissor approximation) for thiol-/amine-linked benzene/Au: the GW self-energy corrects the DFT conductance overestimate and justifies approximating $\\Sigma$ as a static, orbital-diagonal shift — exactly our level-1 δH. DOI 10.1103/PhysRevB.83.115108 δH mechanism — SIESTA 5.2 TBtrans reference · sisl write_delta · sisl #129"}, {"p": "methods/index.html", "t": "Methods", "x": "Methods Computational setup for the current iteration DFT (SIESTA) Functional: vdW-DF2 ( XC.authors LMKLL ) — production for chain and junction relaxation, bands, PDOS. PBE+D2 was anomalously unstable for the Hf₂Se₉ relaxation, so it was dropped ( 02. Hf₂Se₉ stability ). Basis / grid: MeshCutoff 500 Ry; SCF.DM.Tolerance 1.0×10⁻⁷; density mixer weight 0.2, history 15. Transport (TranSIESTA / TBtrans) ~220-atom device (scattering atoms 13–208): VSe₃ buffer + Hf₂Se₉ + VSe₃ buffer between bulk VSe₃ electrodes. Zero-bias NEGF; electrode k = 100 along transport; broadening η = 0.005 eV . Transmission $T(E)$ via sisl from trans.TBT.nc ; eigenchannels (Re/Im/|ψ|² cubes + z-profile). Molecular-level correction (DFT+Σ) The GGA/vdW-DF level underestimates the molecular gap, mis-placing the resonance at $E_F$. Orbital shifts from the vacuum molecule, $\\Delta_i=\\varepsilon_i^{\\text{B3LYP}}-\\varepsilon_i^{\\text{PBE}}$ (PBE in SIESTA vs B3LYP in VASP, vacuum-level aligned), are applied to the device Hamiltonian as a projector on the Hf/Se block, $\\displaystyle (\\Delta H)_{\\mu\\nu}=\\sum_i \\Delta_i\\,(C^i_\\mu)^{*}C^i_\\nu, \\qquad (\\mu,\\nu)\\in\\text{Hf}_2\\text{Se}_9,$ implemented through TBtrans's built-in δH (a deltaH.dH.nc file) with a ±1–2 eV energy window — no TranSIESTA source patch."}, {"p": "motivation/index.html", "t": "Motivation — a single-molecule junction grown inside a nanotube", "x": "Motivation — a single-molecule junction grown inside a nanotube Collaboration experiment (manuscript in preparation) — this page records the experimental findings that motivate the calculation, not our results . Formal citation goes to References on publication. 1. Background — the missing selenium member Transition-metal trichalcogenides (TMTCs, MX₃) are quasi-1D crystals of weakly coupled atomic chains. In the single-chain limit the tellurium members (VTe₃, NbTe₃) are known to take trigonal antiprismatic (TAP) coordination — but the selenium counterpart VSe₃ had never been isolated as a single chain. 2. The experiment Single-chain VSe₃ realized inside carbon nanotubes. Nanotube-confined growth stabilizes the otherwise inaccessible isolated chain. STEM identifies it as trigonal prismatic (TP) — like NbSe₃, unlike the TAP tellurides. This completes the missing Se member of the group-V TMTC single-chain family and establishes a chalcogen-dependent coordination trend under 1D confinement (DFT on local coordination units confirms the TP preference — 01. VSe₃ TP vs TAP ). Beyond the pristine chain: the confined channel also shows atomic-scale Hf incorporation (Z-contrast ADF-STEM) and the formation of Se-rich molecular species — in particular a Hf₂Se₉ single molecule sitting between two VSe₃ single chains : a naturally formed 1D heterojunction at the single-chain limit (manuscript Fig. 5, experimental + simulated STEM + atomic model). 3. Our question That Figure-5 motif — metal chain / single molecule / metal chain — is a molecular junction the experiment can grow but not measure electrically. This project computes its conductance from first principles: does current flow through the Hf₂Se₉ molecule, through which orbital, and where do its levels really sit? Fig. 1. Our DFT model of the observed motif — a Hf₂Se₉ molecule bridging two H-passivated VSe₃ chains (central region of the relaxed transport device, vdW-DF2). Experimental STEM images stay unpublished until the manuscript is out. 4. From the experiment to the results Question raised by the experiment Where it is answered Is TP really the stable coordination of the VSe₃ single chain? 01. VSe₃ TP vs TAP — TP more stable by 0.163 eV/formula, metallic. Is the Hf₂Se₉ bridge structurally consistent with TEM (Hf–Hf ≈ 3.6 Å)? 02. Hf₂Se₉ stability — functional scan against the TEM bond length. Does current actually flow through the molecule? 06. Transmission — three-way $T(E_F)$ 0.70 / 0.35 / 0; the molecule is the sole bridge. Where do the molecular levels really sit (the DFT gap is far too small)? 08. Level correction (DFT+Σ) — gap 0.02 → 0.75 eV, $T(E_F)$ 0.355 → 0.051."}, {"p": "plan/index.html", "t": "Plan", "x": "Plan Current goal · DFT+Σ workflow status · validation gates · figures · queue Current goal The B3LYP DFT+Σ molecular-level correction is applied on the length-converged 9-cell device ($T(E_F)\\,0.355\\to0.051$ — Results: level correction , Section C). Immediate task: the corrected Landauer I–V (zero-bias $T(E)$) (Landauer integral of the corrected $T(E)$) with the bias-window figure. Queued calculations: the true finite-bias chain (TranSIESTA 0 → ±0.5 V with TBT.dH ) and the interface charge transfer $\\Delta\\rho$. Method detail: Methods: DFT+Σ . DFT+Σ workflow — status Step Task Status 1 vacuum molecule (VASP PBE/B3LYP) → per-orbital $\\Delta_i=\\varepsilon_i(\\text{B3LYP})-\\varepsilon_i(\\text{vdW-DF2})$ done 2 SIESTA single-molecule (same PAO basis) → MO coefficients $C$ done ($CSC^\\dagger$ orthonormality 1e−5) 3 build $\\Delta H = S\\,C\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta_i)\\,C^\\dagger S$ on the Hf/Se molecular block, apply to the device (TBtrans δH) done (isolated-molecule projector, Kabsch + Hungarian atom matching) 4 corrected $T(E)$, before/after comparison done — N25_9uc $0.355\\to0.051$; cross-check N25_k100 $0.354\\to0.046$ — document the modified files / formula / application flow (advisor's emphasis) done → Methods: DFT+Σ Validation gates STEP 2: the SIESTA single-molecule PAO basis must match the device molecular basis (same species and basis set). STEP 3: supercell handled ( set_nsc([1,1,1]) ), overlap $S$ on both sides of ΔH, and the $E_F\\pm2$ eV energy-window cutoff applied. STEP 4: TBtrans stdout logs that it read the δH file; the molecular PDOS / MPSH peaks actually move by $\\Delta_i$; and $T(E_F)$ drops as expected (resonance pushed off $E_F$). Figure plan Figure Content Fig 1 Hf₂Se₉ bridging two VSe₃ chains in a CNT; STEM confirming TP VSe₃. Fig 2 VSe₃ metallic band + Hf₂Se₉ HOMO/LUMO wavefunctions. Fig 3 Three-way T(E): pristine / heterojunction / disconnected + eigenchannel. Fig 4 B3LYP-corrected T(E) and the I–V curve. Near-term queue Priority Task Status P1 corrected Landauer I–V (zero-bias $T(E)$) + bias-window figure done → level correction Fig. 10–11 P1 uncorrected 9-cell HOMO/LUMO eigenchannel re-check (−0.065 / +0.003 eV) verified ; isosurface PNGs being rendered P2 true finite-bias I–V — TranSIESTA bias chain 0 → ±0.5 V with the corrected $\\Delta H$ ( TBT.dH ) planned (separate bias-chain calculation) P2 charge transfer $\\Delta\\rho$ at the interface planned (next calculation) P2 course-material figures for the Hf₂Se₉–VSe₃ junction (on request) standby — STEP 2–4 (δH build + corrected $T(E)$); LUMO label wording; 9-UC transport; mid-energy eigenchannels; 3-way disconnected display done (2026-07-01 – 07-02)"}, {"p": "references/index.html", "t": "References", "x": "References Every external reference used on this site: full citation + link, what it contains, and where we used it . Grouped by topic. A. DFT+Σ molecular-level correction J. B. Neaton, M. S. Hybertsen, S. G. Louie , Phys. Rev. Lett. 97 , 216405 (2006). DOI 10.1103/PhysRevLett.97.216405 Origin of the correction. GW shows the metal-surface image charge renormalizes the molecular HOMO–LUMO gap relative to gas phase — an interface polarization effect semi-local DFT misses. Used in: physical basis of our level correction ( Methods: DFT+Σ ); direction-of-gap-change check in Results: level correction . S. Y. Quek, L. Venkataraman, H. J. Choi, S. G. Louie, M. S. Hybertsen, J. B. Neaton , Nano Lett. 7 , 3477 (2007). DOI 10.1021/nl072058i The DFT+Σ transport recipe. Gas-phase gap correction + image-charge scissor for benzenediamine/Au; bare DFT overestimates the conductance. Used in: direct template for our per-orbital scissor $\\Delta_i$ ( Methods: DFT+Σ , Section B of level correction ). M. Strange, C. Rostgaard, H. Häkkinen, K. S. Thygesen , Phys. Rev. B 83 , 115108 (2011). DOI 10.1103/PhysRevB.83.115108 Validation against full GW. Self-consistent GW transport (no scissor approximation) for thiol-/amine-linked benzene/Au; justifies approximating $\\Sigma$ as a static, orbital-diagonal shift. Used in: justification of our static level-1 δH ( Methods: DFT+Σ ). F. Montes, H. Vázquez , Appl. Sci. 11 , 802 (2021). DOI 10.3390/app11020802 Original SIESTA/TranSIESTA implementation — the self-energy correction added to the Hamiltonian as an orbital-dependent operator (benzenediamine/Au; DFT overestimates conductance 5–13×, DFT+Σ brings it to 1.0–1.8× of experiment). Full text not accessed — cited via Fallaque. Used in: implementation precedent and the expected size of the conductance drop (Section C discussion of level correction ). Fallaque et al. , J. Phys.: Condens. Matter 34 , 435901 (2022). DOI 10.1088/1361-648X/ac89c4 Efficient TranSIESTA re-implementation with an infinite-reflection image charge, benchmarked on acene/Au (full text verified). Its workflow — extract $H_\\text{mol}, S_\\text{mol}$ from .TSHS (sisl) → MPSH — is the procedure we adapted; we replaced the MPSH projector with isolated-molecule orbitals because our molecule is covalently fused into the chain. Used in: workflow template + MPSH-vs-isolated-projector decision ( Methods: DFT+Σ , Log 2026-07-02 ). B. Method / software documentation M. Paulsson, M. Brandbyge , Phys. Rev. B 76 , 115117 (2007). DOI 10.1103/PhysRevB.76.115117 Transmission eigenchannels from nonequilibrium Green's functions — the eigenchannel construction (spectral function → $\\tau_n$ decomposition) our eigenchannel figures follow. Used in: eigenchannel sections of transmission and level correction . SIESTA 5.2 TBtrans reference — docs.siesta-project.org The δH mechanism: $H \\leftarrow H + \\delta H$ via TBT.dH , no source patch. Used in: how ΔH enters the device Green function ( Methods: DFT+Σ ). sisl write_delta — sisl.io.tbtrans.delta · issue #129 The level-1 (E- and k-independent) deltaH.dH.nc writer/reader matching TBT.dH . Used in: building our ΔH file ( Methods: DFT+Σ )."}, {"p": "results/h-termination/index.html", "t": "04. H termination of the VSe₃ interface: separation minimum restored", "x": "04. H termination of the VSe₃ interface: separation minimum restored 2026-04-16 · Source: reports/h-termination/ (progress-update.md, h-termination.md, README.md), meeting 2026-04-16. Goal Test the hypothesis (proposed at the 2026-04-07 meeting) that the missing equilibrium in the bare VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉ separation curve is caused by dangling bonds on the cut VSe₃ interface Se . In the TP chain each Se bonds to two V (V–Se = 2.508 Å); cutting the chain removes one V, leaving an unsaturated bond that binds unphysically strongly to Hf₂Se₉. Hf₂Se₉ is closed-shell, so the problem is localized to the VSe₃ side. Verification: attach H to the VSe₃ interface Se and compare the separation-energy curve with the bare case. Method H attached to the 3 interface Se on each side (6 H total), VSe₃ side only. H placed toward the missing-V direction (Se–H bond aligned with the original V–Se direction). d(Se–H) = 1.46 Å (H₂Se experimental r₀, NIST CCCBDB). Single-point scan d = 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 3.5, 4.0, 4.5 Å. vdW-DF2 (LMKLL), MeshCutoff 500 Ry, k = 1×1×4, 44 atoms (6 V + 2 Hf + 30 Se + 6 H). Here d is the z-distance between the VSe₃ terminal Se and the Hf₂Se₉ terminal Se. Result With H, a clear separation-energy minimum appears at d = 3.5 Å — matching the experimental ~3.5 Å. The bare structure keeps decreasing monotonically. d (Å) E sep , with H (eV) E sep , without H (eV) 2.0 +26.90 — 2.5 +4.92 −6.21 3.0 +0.15 −4.10 3.5 −0.68 (min) −1.61 4.0 −0.48 0.00 4.5 0.00 (ref) — \"Without H\" = 08-hetero-v2 (vdW-DF2 symmetric), referenced to d = 4.0 Å. \"With H\" referenced to d = 4.5 Å. Absolute Kohn–Sham energies are in scripts/plot_sep_energy.py . Fig. 1. Separation energy vs interface separation: H-terminated (with H) develops a minimum at d = 3.5 Å (circled); the bare structure (without H) does not. Green band = experimental separation. (Plot: scripts/plot_sep_energy.py .) H-orientation comparison (2026-04-16) Three initial H directions were compared under the same conditions (vdW-DF2 single-point, 44 atoms, Se–H = 1.46 Å): bond (H toward the missing V center), gap (H ⊥ Se₃ plane, i.e. pure ±z), and tilt (H tilted 45° radially outward). gap is the most stable over the whole range; its minimum sits at d = 3.5–4.0 Å (the two points differ by ~4 meV). bond is 1.11 eV higher and tilt 3.72 eV higher than gap . Recommended relaxation starting structure: gap mode, d = 3.5 Å. Fig. 2. Schematic of the three H-orientation modes (bond / gap / tilt). Fig. 3. Separation-energy scan for the three H-orientation modes. Junction configurations (2026-06-12) The H-passivated VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉–VSe₃ junction in the three modes; all three minimise at d ≈ 3.5 Å, with top the most stable. Fig. 4a. bond (top) configuration. Fig. 4b. gap (hollow) configuration. Fig. 4c. tilt configuration. Fig. 5. ΔE vs Se–Se separation d for the three modes (top / hollow / tilted); minimum at d ≈ 3.5 Å. Discussion H passivation removes the interface's unphysical strong binding: d < 3.0 Å rises steeply (Pauli repulsion / repulsive wall), d ≈ 3.5 Å balances vdW attraction and repulsion (equilibrium), d > 4.0 Å converges. This indicates the VSe₃ Se dangling bond was the main cause of the earlier separation failure. The H-direction choice is supported by MoS₂-edge literature (Bollinger 2003, Kronberg 2017, Lauritsen 2018): the chalcogen–H bond points along the dangling-bond direction, not toward the metal center; the ~1 eV mode spread is consistent with those studies. Next steps (as stated) Finalize H direction (bond/gap/tilt comparison). Full relaxation at transport size (20 unit cells). Hf₂Se₉ PDOS → band gap. TranSIESTA transport (electrode + device + I-V)."}, {"p": "results/hetero-separation/index.html", "t": "03. VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉ heterostructure separation test", "x": "03. VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉ heterostructure separation test 2026-04-07 · Source: reports/initial-structures/failure-analysis.md §6, hetero-energy-vs-sep.csv, meeting 2026-04-07. Goal In the VSe₃ (3 unit cells)–Hf₂Se₉–VSe₃ (3 unit cells) heterojunction, compute the single-point energy as a function of the VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉ interface separation d (2.5–4.0 Å), to find the equilibrium interface distance and check whether DFT reproduces the experimentally observed ~3.5 Å minimum. Method Three versions were computed. Because their structures differ, total energy cannot be compared directly between versions; only the energy trend within each version is compared, with d = 4.0 Å as the per-version reference. Version Functional Structure origin Interface arrangement 07-hetero vdW-DF2 vdW-DF2 relaxed V-Se₃-V (asymmetric) 08-hetero-v2 vdW-DF2 same as 07, flipped so Se faces Se Se₃-V-Se₃ (symmetric) 09-hetero-d2 PBE+D2 PBE+D2 relaxed Se₃-V-Se₃ (symmetric) 07 vs 08: same functional, same structure — interface arrangement only (V-facing vs Se-facing). Total energy is directly comparable. 07/08 vs 09: functional and relaxed structure both differ — total energy not directly comparable. Result All three decrease monotonically over 2.5–4.0 Å — no minimum within the scanned range. d (Å) 07 vdW-DF2 V-Se₃-V (eV) 08 vdW-DF2 Se₃-V-Se₃ (eV) 09 PBE+D2 Se₃-V-Se₃ (eV) 2.5 −3.73 −6.21 −8.31 3.0 −2.00 −4.10 −4.89 3.5 −0.84 −1.61 −1.84 4.0 0.00 0.00 0.00 Energies relative to d = 4.0 Å within each version (data: data/hetero-energy-vs-sep.csv , with absolute E KS ). Fig. 1. Relative energy vs interface separation for the three versions (each referenced to its own d = 4.0 Å). Fig. 2. Side views at d = 3.5 Å: 07 (vdW-DF2, V-Se₃-V asymmetric), 08 (vdW-DF2, Se₃-V-Se₃ symmetric), 09 (PBE+D2, Se₃-V-Se₃ symmetric). Discussion All three keep decreasing at d = 2.5 Å — no minimum is found within 2.5–4.0 Å. 07 vs 08 (same functional, interface only): the Se–Se symmetric arrangement (08) has a steeper slope than V-facing (07), and its total energy at d = 4.0 Å is 0.60 eV lower — i.e. the Se–Se interface binds more strongly. 09 (PBE+D2) cannot be compared directly to 07/08 because both functional and relaxed structure differ. This monotonic behavior motivated the dangling-bond hypothesis and the H-termination test (see H termination ), where passivating the VSe₃ interface Se restores a minimum near d ≈ 3.5 Å."}, {"p": "results/hf2se9-stability/index.html", "t": "02. Hf₂Se₉ relaxation stability: functional & vdW scan", "x": "02. Hf₂Se₉ relaxation stability: functional & vdW scan 2026-04-07 · Source: reports/hf2se9-stability/ (+ docs/), reports/initial-structures/failure-analysis.md, meetings 2026-03-19/04-07. Goal Find a DFT setting under which Hf₂Se₉ molecule relaxation converges stably and reproduces the experimental structure. Prior calculations repeatedly failed (cell expansion 16–53%, structure collapse). Four axes are examined independently to isolate the cause: exchange–correlation functional, basis set, DFT+U, and CNT confinement. The test system is the Hf₂Se₉ molecule (11 atoms, 25 Å cubic cell, Γ-only) — the lightest case, used to scan settings quickly. Pass/fail criteria (TEM reference Hf–Hf = 3.6 Å) Criterion Success Failure Hf–Hf distance 3.5–3.8 Å held < 3.0 Å (collapse) or > 5.0 Å (decomposition) Force convergence < 0.01 eV/Å not converged (> 1000 steps) Structure confacial bioctahedron held Se₃ triangle distorted, Hf escapes Experimental agreement Hf–Hf deviation < 5% deviation > 10% Method — Phase A: exchange-correlation + vdW scan Fixed: basis EnergyShift 0.01 Ry (136 meV), U = 0, no CNT. Variable: functional + vdW method. # Functional vdW Code Purpose A1 PBE none SIESTA baseline — does structure hold without vdW? A2 PBE + Grimme D2 fdf2grimme SIESTA reproduce previous setup A3 vdW-DF2 (LMKLL) built-in SIESTA revPBE exchange, non-local correlation A4 PBE + D3(BJ) DFT-D3 SIESTA coordination-number-dependent C₆ A5 PBE + D3(BJ) IVDW=12 VASP plane-wave reference — isolate NAO-basis issue A6 HSE06 + D3(BJ) IVDW=12 VASP hybrid functional — exchange accuracy Result Phase A — Hf–Hf distances Test Functional Basis Code Hf–Hf (Å) Δ(3.6 Å) Converged Note A1 PBE 136 meV SIESTA 3.857 +7% ✅ 14 steps holds even without vdW A2 PBE+D2 136 meV SIESTA — — ❌ SCF not converged (3 failures, cause not identified) A3 vdW-DF2 136 meV SIESTA 3.967 +10% ✅ 13 steps revPBE exchange A4 PBE+D3(BJ) 136 meV SIESTA 3.857 +7% ✅ 14 steps = A1 (D3 has no effect on an isolated molecule) A5 PBE+D3 PAW VASP 2.934 −18.5% ✅ plane-wave reference A6 HSE06+D3 PAW VASP 3.330 −7.5% ✅ closest overall Within SIESTA, PBE and PBE+D3(BJ) (3.857 Å) are closest to experiment; vdW-DF2 over-expands further (3.967 Å). VASP HSE06+D3 (3.330 Å) is closest of all, but the SIESTA↔VASP discrepancy is large. A2 (PBE+D2) repeatedly fails SCF convergence — cause not yet isolated. Fig. 1. Relaxed Hf₂Se₉ molecule: A1 (SIESTA PBE), A3 (SIESTA vdW-DF2), A5 (VASP PBE+D3), A6 (VASP HSE06+D3). Molecule vs chain (vdW-DF2) Molecule (05) Chain (06) Initial Hf–Hf 3.62 Å 3.62 Å Relaxed Hf–Hf 3.967 Å (+10%) 4.164 Å (+15.7%) vdW gap (chain) — 5.89 Å (+68%) Force 0.027 eV/Å (≈ converged) 0.39 eV/Å (unstable) Both over-expand relative to experiment (3.6 Å); the chain is worse because periodic vdW interactions accumulate and revPBE's extra repulsion accelerates the expansion. A separate PBE+D2 chain run reached 197 steps before OOM (Hf–Hf = 4.47 Å, max force = 0.094 eV/Å, not converged). Fig. 2. vdW-DF2 relaxed Hf₂Se₉ molecule (left) and chain (right). Why prior relaxations failed (archive analysis) Six standalone Hf₂Se₉-chain relaxation attempts in the archive: Attempt Condition Result 001_rlx neutral, D2, EnergyShift 10 meV c-axis +16% expansion 002_totcharge-2 charge −2 still expanding 003_originstruct 1-unit (11 atoms) c-axis −22% contraction (opposite direction) 005_basis50 EnergyShift 50 meV nearly unchanged (convergence unclear) 006_basis50_tot-2 50 meV + charge −2 c-axis +53% → chain decomposes Identified root causes: Grimme D2 Hf C₆ inaccuracy. C₆ sets the vdW attraction strength ($E_\\mathrm{disp} = -C_6/R^6$). D2 uses a fixed free-atom value; free Hf has C₆ = 1089.41 eV·Å⁶, but inside Hf₂Se₉ the Hf is surrounded by 6 Se, so its real C₆ should be much smaller. D2 ignores this environment dependence. D3(BJ) interpolates C₆ from the coordination number, fixing it; vdW-DF2 avoids empirical parameters entirely (vdW energy from the electron density). Basis-set sensitivity (BSSE). Large basis (10 meV) → 16% expansion; small basis (50 meV) → nearly unchanged. The vdW-gap energy (~tens of meV) is within the BSSE error. MD.VariableCell = T. Allowing c-axis relaxation lets the flat PES collapse or expand the vdW gap under tiny forces. Starting-structure dependence. The 2-unit-cell start expands; the 1-unit-cell bulk-extracted start contracts — opposite directions for the same system. Heterostructure \"false convergence.\" In the heterojunction with 3 VSe₃ unit cells per lead, 1-step convergence is a VSe₃ cage effect (kinetic trapping), not a true minimum. By contrast VASP MD with DFT-D3 held Hf–Hf = 3.82 Å. vdW-correction comparison: D2 vs D3(BJ) vs vdW-DF2 Grimme D2 (Grimme 2006) adds dispersion on top of PBE: $$E_\\mathrm{disp} = -s_6 \\sum_{i with element-fixed C₆ (no environment dependence) and PBE exchange (s₆ = 0.75 for PBE). D3(BJ) (Grimme 2010) interpolates C₆ from the coordination number and adds a C₈ term with Becke–Johnson damping. vdW-DF2 (Lee 2010) is non-empirical: $$E_\\mathrm{vdW\\text{-}DF2} = E_x^{\\mathrm{revPBE}} + E_c^{\\mathrm{LDA}} + E_c^{\\mathrm{nl}},\\qquad E_c^{\\mathrm{nl}} = \\tfrac12 \\iint n(\\mathbf{r})\\,\\Phi(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{r}')\\,n(\\mathbf{r}')\\,d\\mathbf{r}\\,d\\mathbf{r}'$$ where the non-local correlation $E_c^{\\mathrm{nl}}$ is computed directly from the electron density $n(\\mathbf{r})$ with kernel $\\Phi$, and uses revPBE exchange (more repulsive than PBE). Symbols: $C_6^{ij}$ = pairwise dispersion coefficient, $r_{ij}$ = interatomic distance, $f_\\mathrm{damp}$ = short-range damping, $s_6$ = global scaling. Feature D2 D3(BJ) vdW-DF2 C₆ determination fixed (1 per element) CN-dependent interpolation direct from density Environment adaptivity none via CN fully self-consistent Exchange functional PBE PBE revPBE damping zero (discontinuous) BJ (smooth) not needed (seamless) SIESTA support built-in (grimme.fdf) conditional (libdftd3; not in 5.2.x) built-in (XC.functional VDW) Reliability for Hf₂Se₉ low (failures observed) high (VASP-validated) high (theoretical) Comparison methodology (how \"better\" is judged) Total energy cannot be compared across functionals — each approximates $E_{xc}$ differently and uses a different energy reference (e.g. vdW-DF2 uses revPBE, D2 uses PBE). The valid comparisons are: (1) relaxed structure parameters vs experiment (most important — Hf–Hf vs TEM 3.6 Å); (2) relative energies within the same functional (e.g. the 41 meV/atom TP–TAP split in vdW-DF2); (3) binding/cohesive energy within a functional; and (4) convergence and structural stability. Phase C — CNT confinement (plan) Experimentally Hf₂Se₉ is synthesized/observed inside a CNT. The Phase C question is whether the CNT actually stabilizes the geometry toward the experimental Hf–Hf, or merely traps a metastable geometry by preventing motion. The first run is planned as \"CNT atoms fixed, Hf₂Se₉ relaxed only\", following the JACS 2021 NbTe₃/VTe₃/TiTe₃@CNT protocol (10.1021/jacs.0c10175). A diameter scan over CNT chiralities (≈ 12 Å inner diameter needed; (9,9) and (16,0) as starting candidates) precedes relaxation and MD. Status: structure scaffolds only; a (9,9)+Hf₂Se₉ assembly was generated. Fig. 3. Hf₂Se₉ in a (9,9) CNT, side view (assembled structure; calculation not yet run). Discussion / next steps Confirm optimal XC + EnergyShift (Phase A/B), then DFT+U scan (Phase D), then CNT (Phase C). Resolve A2 (PBE+D2) SCF failure; obtain QE A7 to separate code dependence. Validate the chosen functional on the chain, since D3 has no effect on an isolated molecule."}, {"p": "results/index.html", "t": "Results", "x": "Results What we have established, by topic The headline: a single Hf₂Se₉ molecule bridges two metallic VSe₃ chains and is the sole conduction path — removing it kills the conductance. Conduction is resonant because the molecular frontier levels sit on $E_F$, which is exactly why the level alignment now needs correcting. The findings, one page each: Date Topic Key result 2026-04-07 01. VSe₃ TP vs TAP TP more stable by 0.163 eV/formula; metallic 1D lead. 2026-04-07 02. Hf₂Se₉ stability PBE+D2 anomalous; vdW-DF2 settled as production. 2026-04-07 03. Hetero separation VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉ separation vs energy. 2026-04-16 04. H-termination Interface H modes; separation minimum restored. 2026-06-12 05. Junction structure 60° staggered (43.3 meV); length-converged by 9 VSe₃ unit cells per electrode. 2026-06-12 06. Transmission $T(E_F)$ = 0.70 / 0.35 / 0 (pristine / hetero / disconnected); channel $\\tau_0\\approx1$. 2026-06-12 07. Hf₂Se₉ molecule Gap ≈ 0.024 eV (PBE) → 0.752 eV (B3LYP); HOMO/LUMO delocalised — drives the level correction. 2026-06-26 · now 08. Level correction (DFT+Σ) DFT+Σ applied on the 9-cell device: $T(E_F)$ 0.355 → 0.051, channel moves to the HOMO resonance; finite-bias I–V next. Production functional is vdW-DF2 (PBE+D2 was anomalous); full setup on Methods . The B3LYP DFT+Σ level correction and the finite-bias I–V are in progress (see Methods / Plan). How we got here — topic reports The chronological record behind the results above (full text in reports/<NN>-<topic>/ in the repo). Stage 1 — Structure & stability (01–05) # Topic Finding 01 previous-calculations Review at project start. 02 hf2se9-stability Plan to fix the correct Hf₂Se₉ set-up. 03 initial-structures Relaxation-failure analysis + initial structures. 04 h-termination H-termination of the heterostructure. 05 d2-vs-d3 dispersion PBE+D2 anomalous — the dispersion-correction analysis behind the vdW-DF2 choice. Stage 2 — Transport set-up (06–09) # Topic Finding 06 perturbation-range Macroscopic-averaged ΔV measurement plan. 07 transport-workflow 1D heterojunction transport / I–V workflow. 08 reference-2nd Second reference round. 09 perturbation-range2 Perturbation range re-measured (v3 base). Stage 3 — Transmission physics & convergence (10–15) # Topic Finding 10 transmission-gap-physics Naming + physics of the gap / isolated peak. 11 transport-electrode-lattice-standard Bulk-lead lattice choice (literature). 12 scattering-length-method Scattering-length method. 13 transmission-eta-length Length dependence = η-broadening artifact. 14 tbtrans-contour-eta TBtrans contour (Simpson) + η, from the manual. 15 charge-transfer-method Charge-transfer (Δρ) extraction method."}, {"p": "results/junction/index.html", "t": "05. Junction structure — 60° staggered, length-converged", "x": "05. Junction structure — 60° staggered, length-converged 2026-06-12 · Assembling and settling the Hf₂Se₉–VSe₃ device Goal Build a relaxed, converged scattering region: how the molecule sits between the chains (orientation, separation) and how much VSe₃ buffer the transport needs. Method SIESTA, vdW-DF2; H-passivated interface; orientation and lateral spacing relaxed; scattering-region buffer scanned 3→5→7→9 unit cells per side. Result Fig. 1. Relaxed 60° junction (H-termination v3, cell-fixed): one Se-capped VSe₃–Hf₂Se₉–VSe₃ repeat, 43 atoms (cyan Hf, grey V, orange Se, white H). Interactive (drag to rotate, scroll to zoom) — the same relaxed 60° structure: V grey · Se orange · Hf · H. Renders via the portal / qmp-site serve (3Dmol from assets/lib/ ); a static PNG is above for offline viewing. Dispersion correction: PBE+D2 was anomalously unstable for the Hf₂Se₉ relaxation; vdW-DF2 (production) converges cleanly. Orientation: 60° staggered registry favoured by 43.3 meV (angle and lateral spacing both relaxed); H-passivation minimum at d ≈ 3.5 Å. Length convergence: the molecule–lead end gap settles once the VSe₃ buffer reaches 9 unit cells per electrode — 3.633 → 3.583 → 3.575 → 3.576 Å (increments 50 → 8 → 1 mÅ), and $T(E_F)$ is unchanged between 3 and 9 unit cells per electrode (0.354 vs 0.348). VSe₃ unit cells per electrode 3 5 7 9 molecule–lead end gap (Å) 3.633 3.583 3.575 3.576 Interface rotation The central Hf₂Se₉ was rotated in 15° increments (0°–180°). ΔE minimises at 60° and 180° ; the 60° staggered registry is 43.3 meV more stable than 0° (Se-aligned). 0° (Se-aligned) 15° 30° 60° (staggered, most stable) Fig. 2. Rotation structures 0° / 15° / 30° / 60° (presentation renders, along the chain axis). The same relaxed structures, interactive (drag to rotate, scroll to zoom): 0° (Se-aligned) 15° 30° 60° (staggered, most stable) Fig. 3. Relaxed interface at four rotation angles — interactive (cell-fixed, H-term v3). Source: 10-hetero-h-term-v3-relax/cellfix/rot_0NN . Fig. 4. ΔE vs rotation angle (0°–180°, 15° steps) — minima at 60° and 180°; 60° is 43.3 meV below 0°. Molecular PDOS This PDOS is for the 60° (staggered) junction — the most stable registry. Its structure (the same one rotated to 60°): Fig. 5. The 60° relaxed junction whose PDOS is shown below. Fig. 6. PDOS projected on the central Hf₂Se₉ of the 60° junction (structure in Fig. 5): molecular states concentrate at and below $E_F$, Se-dominated. Discussion The device geometry is settled, so transmission differences come from electronic structure, not an under-converged cell. Per advisor request the production transport is being re-run on a structure relaxed with 9 VSe₃ unit cells per electrode (electrode-only extension) as a final check."}, {"p": "results/level-correction/index.html", "t": "08. Molecular-level correction (DFT+Σ) and length convergence", "x": "08. Molecular-level correction (DFT+Σ) and length convergence 2026-06-26 · current frontier · A longer scattering region confirms the result; B3LYP orbital shifts computed as the input to the DFT+Σ level correction Goal Two things before the level-corrected transport: (1) confirm the conductance is converged once the scattering region (the central device between the two semi-infinite VSe₃ electrodes) is made longer, and (2) compute the B3LYP→vdW-DF2 molecular-orbital energy shifts $\\Delta_i$ that feed the DFT+Σ correction. A. Scattering-region length convergence The 60° heterojunction was relaxed and its transport recomputed with the scattering region extended from 3 to 9 VSe₃ unit cells per electrode (cell-fixed relaxation, vdW-DF2; transport at electrode k-point grid 100, η = 0.005 eV). Geometry, molecular PDOS, and transmission all converge. Relaxation — interface geometry converges by 7 unit cells The interface bond lengths and the molecule–chain gap stop changing by 7 cells: the molecule–chain (Se···VSe₃) gap moves 50 → 8 → 1 mÅ and is stable at 9. Interactive — the relaxed 9-unit-cell junction (semi-infinite VSe₃ electrode → H-terminated Hf₂Se₉ bridge → VSe₃; 92 atoms, $L_z$ = 69.9 Å; drag to rotate, scroll to zoom). Relaxed coordinates (vdW-DF2, cell-fixed) from 10-hetero-h-term-v3-relax/01_relax/rot_060_tight_9uc . VSe₃ unit cells / electrode Hf–Se (Å) H–Se (Å) Se···VSe₃ gap (Å) 3 2.620 1.516 3.633 5 2.619 1.515 3.583 7 2.621 1.516 3.575 9 2.621 1.516 3.576 Fig. 1. Interface structural metrics vs scattering-region length — flat from 7 to 9 unit cells. (Relaxed structures in 10-hetero-h-term-v3-relax/01_relax .) Molecular PDOS is length-independent The molecular Hf₂Se₉ PDOS is nearly identical across 3 / 5 / 7 / 9 unit cells — the peak positions do not move, confirming length-independent molecular levels. Fig. 2. Molecular PDOS overlaid for 3 / 5 / 7 / 9 unit cells per electrode — peak positions unchanged. Transmission is converged The Fermi-level transmission is unchanged — $T(E_F) = \\mathbf{0.348}$ for 9 cells vs 0.354 for 3 — with a single dominant eigenchannel; the Landauer I–V (zero-bias $T(E)$) shows no negative differential conductance (resonant tunnelling). VSe₃ unit cells / electrode $T(E_F)$ (η = 0.005 eV) transmission eigenchannel 3 0.354 — 9 0.348 $\\tau_0 = 0.989$ (single channel) Eigenchannel vs energy — resonant tunnelling Real-space $|\\psi|^2$ isosurface of the transmission eigenchannel at four energies. At $E_F$ the channel is delocalised across the molecule (on-resonance); moving away in energy it becomes localised / decays at the molecule (off-resonance) — the signature of resonant tunnelling through the molecular level. $E_F$ — delocalised (on-resonance) +0.10 eV +0.19 eV +0.30 eV — localised (off-resonance) Fig. 3. Transmitting eigenchannel $|\\psi|^2$ at $E_F$ → +0.30 eV: delocalised through the molecule on-resonance, localised off-resonance. Fig. 4. Landauer I–V (zero-bias $T(E)$) (9-unit-cell device) — resonant tunnelling, no negative differential conductance. B. B3LYP molecular-level correction (DFT+Σ) — inputs The semi-local functional (vdW-DF2) underestimates the molecular gap, so the transmission resonance is mis-placed at $E_F$. The correction is a DFT+Σ level alignment : each molecular orbital is shifted by $\\Delta_i = \\varepsilon_i^{\\text{B3LYP}} - \\varepsilon_i^{\\text{vdW-DF2}}$ and added to the device Hamiltonian as $\\displaystyle \\Delta H = S\\,C\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta_i)\\,C^{\\dagger}\\,S,$ with $S$ the overlap and $C$ the isolated-molecule MO coefficients — the in-device molecular block has no clean molecular levels here (Hf₂Se₉ is covalently fused into the chain), so an MPSH projector cannot be used; construction detail in Methods: DFT+Σ . From the vacuum molecule (vdW-DF2 in SIESTA vs B3LYP in VASP, vacuum-level aligned) the gap opens 0.02 → 0.75 eV with a strongly asymmetric shift — occupied levels drop ≈ 1 eV ($\\Delta_\\text{HOMO} = -1.01$ eV) but virtual levels only ≈ 0.1–0.3 eV ($\\Delta_\\text{LUMO} = -0.29$ eV). The device transport uses the same vdW-DF2, so the correction functional is consistent. Fig. 5. (a) Level alignment — solid = occupied, dashed = virtual; (b) per-orbital shift $\\Delta_i$. Occupied levels drop ~1 eV, virtual only ~0.1–0.3 eV. Full set of levels (vacuum-aligned, $E - V_\\text{vac}$, eV; mo_delta_orbital.csv ): Orbital vdW-DF2 (SIESTA) B3LYP (VASP) Δ = B3LYP − vdW-DF2 LUMO+4 −5.40 −5.49 −0.09 LUMO+3 −5.40 −5.68 −0.27 LUMO+2 −5.55 −5.71 −0.16 LUMO+1 −5.55 −5.73 −0.18 LUMO −5.55 −5.84 −0.29 HOMO −5.58 −6.59 −1.01 HOMO−1 −5.58 −6.73 −1.15 HOMO−2 −5.75 −6.77 −1.02 HOMO−3 −5.96 −6.80 −0.84 HOMO−4 −6.57 −7.24 −0.67 HOMO−5 −6.58 −7.81 −1.23 HOMO−6 −6.75 −7.85 −1.10 LUMO+5 is excluded — its B3LYP energy (−2.27 eV) sits ~3.5 eV above the other virtual levels: high virtual orbitals are basis-dependent (SIESTA numerical atomic orbitals vs VASP PAW), so the two codes' orbitals stop matching there. It is outside the transport window and is not used in the correction. C. Corrected transmission — DFT+Σ applied We built the correction from the isolated-molecule MO coefficients $C$, projected onto the device molecular block (Hf₂Se₉, atoms 107–117): $$\\Delta H \\;=\\; S\\,C\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta_i)\\,C^{\\dagger}S,$$ written as a level-1 deltaH.dH.nc and applied through TBT.dH (N25_9uc, 0 bias, η = 0.005 eV). at $E_F$ vdW-DF2 DFT+Σ Transmission $T(E_F)$ 0.355 0.051 (~7× ↓) Molecular PDOS (1/eV) 20.1 1.2 (~17× ↓) Molecular gap 0.02 eV 0.75 eV Fig. 6. $T(E)$ (log) — vdW-DF2 vs DFT+Σ (N25_9uc). Opening the molecular gap pushes the frontier resonance off $E_F$, so $T(E_F)$ drops $0.355\\to0.051$. This is the expected DFT+Σ effect (Montes & Vázquez 2021; Fallaque 2022): semi-local DFT overestimates molecular-junction conductance, and the hybrid-level correction brings it down. A single eigenchannel still carries the current ($\\tau_0\\approx0.12$). The same $\\Delta H$ applied to the 3-cell device (N25_k100) gives $0.354\\to0.046$ — the correction transfers across scattering-region lengths. Fig. 7. $T(E)$ (black, log, left) + Hf₂Se₉ molecular PDOS (orange, right) for N25_9uc — vdW-DF2 (left panel) vs DFT+Σ (right panel). Uncorrected: molecular PDOS piles up on $E_F$. Corrected: it is pushed off $E_F$ → PDOS$(E_F)\\ 20.1\\to1.2$ /eV, $T(E_F)\\ 0.355\\to0.051$. Fig. 8. Same, zoomed to $|E-E_F|\\le0.5$ eV. The correction splits the molecular frontier off $E_F$ (HOMO $-0.095$ / LUMO $+0.085$ eV), leaving $E_F$ in a gap — the orange peaks visibly move away from the dotted $E_F$ line. Corrected eigenchannel — the frontier channel after DFT+Σ With the correction applied the frontier levels split off $E_F$ (isolated-molecule HOMO $-0.095$, LUMO $+0.085$ eV), so the transmission eigenchannel is now strongest at the HOMO resonance instead of pinned at $E_F$. Real-space $|\\psi|^2$ isosurface of the transmission eigenchannel EC₀ (corrected $H+\\Delta H$, N25_9uc; V grey, Se green, orange = channel). energy $E-E_F$ (eV) $T$ (η = 1e-6) $\\tau_0$ vdW-DF2 (uncorrected) HOMO −0.065 0.367 0.336 $E_F$ 0 0.991 0.989 LUMO +0.003 0.999 0.998 DFT+Σ (corrected) HOMO −0.095 0.373 0.373 $E_F$ 0 0.124 0.124 LUMO +0.085 0.074 0.074 Before the correction the frontier is pinned at $E_F$ (LUMO at $+0.003$ eV) and the channel at $E_F$ is near-ballistic ($\\tau_0 = 0.989$; its isosurface is the first row of Fig. 3). After the correction the frontier splits off $E_F$ and the $E_F$ channel weight drops to $\\tau_0 = 0.124$ — conduction moves to the HOMO resonance. HOMO resonance, $-0.095$ eV — $\\tau_0 = 0.373$ (strongest) $E_F$ — $\\tau_0 = 0.124$ LUMO resonance, $+0.085$ eV — $\\tau_0 = 0.074$ Fig. 9. Corrected ($H+\\Delta H$) transmission eigenchannel $|\\psi|^2$ at the shifted frontier resonances and $E_F$ (N25_9uc). The channel is strongest at the HOMO resonance; at $E_F$ (now in the opened frontier gap) the weight through the molecule is reduced. Cubes: N25_9uc/0bias/dft_sigma/eigenchannel/{HOMO,EF,LUMO}/EC_0_{Re,Im,psi2}_*.cube . η = 1e-6 (eigenchannel) differs from the transport η = 0.005 that gives $T(E_F)$ (sharp $E_F$ resonance). Corrected Landauer I–V (zero-bias $T(E)$) Landauer integral of the corrected $T(E)$ (same method as the uncorrected I–V; bias-independent $T(E)$ assumed — the true finite-bias chain is queued): $$I(V) \\;=\\; \\frac{2e}{h}\\int T(E)\\,\\big[f(E-\\mu_L)-f(E-\\mu_R)\\big]\\,dE, \\qquad \\mu_L = 0,\\;\\; \\mu_R = -eV,\\;\\; T = 300\\ \\mathrm{K}.$$ The correction suppresses the low-bias current (at $\\pm0.1$ V: $+1.82/-1.59\\to+0.83/-0.32$ μA) and strengthens the asymmetry: at $\\pm0.5$ V $+5.04/-2.70\\to+4.30/-1.04$ μA, rectification ratio $|I_+/I_-|$ 1.9 → 4.1. Fig. 10. Landauer I–V (zero-bias $T(E)$) — vdW-DF2 vs DFT+Σ (N25_9uc). The corrected curve is flatter at low bias ($E_F$ now sits in the opened frontier gap) and more strongly rectifying. Fig. 11. Bias windows ($|V| = 0.1$–$0.5$ V) over the corrected $T(E)$ — the integration ranges behind Fig. 10 ($\\mu_L = 0$ dotted, $\\mu_R = -V$ dashed). Discussion / next What is actually implemented already: TBtrans already has the runtime hook for an additive Hamiltonian/self-energy correction, and sisl already has the file writer for that hook. The official TBtrans manual states that the electronic structure can be changed as $H \\leftarrow H + \\delta H + \\delta\\Sigma$, and defines TBT.dH as the fdf flag for the NetCDF4 file containing the $\\delta H$ data. In sisl, sisl.io.tbtrans.delta implements the matching .dH.nc writer/reader: write_delta stores level-1 static terms when no E or k is supplied, and also supports k-dependent, E-dependent, and E+k-dependent delta files. What is not automatic: there is no project-specific DFT+Σ button that knows our molecular orbitals or B3LYP shifts. We still have to construct the actual molecular projector correction, $\\Delta H = S\\,C\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta_i)\\,C^\\dagger S$, write that matrix with sisl as a level-1 deltaH.dH.nc , then tell TBtrans to use it with TBT.dH deltaH.dH.nc . The source-patch part is unnecessary because the file-based δH insertion path already exists; the physics/mapping step is still our implementation task. Evidence tags: Official TBtrans δ-elements Official TBT.dH flag sisl write_delta source DFT+Σ implementation note No SIESTA source patch needed — the shift applies through TBtrans's built-in δH term ( TBT.dH ) once we provide the correctly constructed deltaH.dH.nc file. Done: $\\Delta H$ built and corrected $T(E)$ computed on the length-converged N25_9uc device (Section C) — $T(E_F)\\,0.355\\to0.051$, molecular PDOS$(E_F)\\,20\\to1.2$/eV. Cross-checked on the 3-cell N25_k100: $0.354\\to0.046$ — the correction transfers directly. Corrected eigenchannel rendered (Fig. 9). Next: finite-bias I–V with the corrected Hamiltonian; charge-transfer $\\Delta\\rho$."}, {"p": "results/molecule/index.html", "t": "07. Hf₂Se₉ molecule — frontier levels at E_F", "x": "07. Hf₂Se₉ molecule — frontier levels at E_F 2026-06-12 · The bridging unit and why its level alignment matters Goal Characterise the Hf₂Se₉ molecule that bridges the two VSe₃ chains: its gap, the spatial character of the frontier orbitals, and how far DFT misplaces them. Method SIESTA, vdW-DF2 relaxation + single point; HOMO/LUMO wavefunctions plotted. A separate vacuum single point compares PBE vs the B3LYP hybrid, vacuum-level aligned. Result Tiny gap: the molecular HOMO–LUMO gap is only ≈ 0.024 eV , so the frontier levels sit essentially on $E_F$ (Fig. 2). Delocalised frontier orbitals: both HOMO and LUMO spread over the whole Hf₂Se₉ unit (Fig. 4) — they can carry current end to end. Hybrid opens the gap: B3LYP widens it to 0.752 eV (vacuum-aligned shifts $\\Delta_\\text{HOMO}=-1.015$, $\\Delta_\\text{LUMO}=-0.286$ eV). Fig. 1. Hf₂Se₉ molecule (2 Hf + 9 Se): side view (left) and top view along the Hf–Hf axis (right). Interactive — the same relaxed Hf₂Se₉ (drag to rotate, scroll to zoom): Fig. 2. Molecular levels near $E_F$ (vdW-DF2): a near-degenerate HOMO/LUMO pair straddling zero — the small-gap origin of the resonance at $E_F$. State $E-E_F$ (eV) LUMO+2 +0.0075 LUMO+1 +0.0040 LUMO +0.0033 HOMO −0.0201 HOMO−1 −0.0202 HOMO−2 −0.1883 Gap = $E_\\text{LUMO}-E_\\text{HOMO}$ = 0.024 eV (vdW-DF2). (Source: 2026-06-12 meeting.) Vacuum-level alignment (B3LYP correction input) To place the molecular levels on an absolute scale, the orbital energies are referenced to the vacuum level $V_\\text{vac}$ taken from the planar-averaged Hartree potential in the vacuum region (Fig. 3). Aligned this way, the hybrid B3LYP opens the gap from 0.023 → 0.752 eV and lowers the HOMO by ~1 eV relative to the LUMO — the input to the DFT+Σ level correction . Fig. 3. Planar-averaged Hartree potential of the vacuum molecule; the plateau sets $V_\\text{vac}$ used to align the levels. Method $V_\\text{vac}$ (eV) HOMO − $V_\\text{vac}$ (eV) LUMO − $V_\\text{vac}$ (eV) gap (eV) vdW-DF2 (SIESTA) 0.020 −5.577 −5.554 0.023 B3LYP (VASP) 0.153 −6.592 −5.840 0.752 Fig. 4. HOMO (left) and LUMO (right) — both delocalised over the molecule (Hf olive, Se green; isosurface sign in cyan/yellow). Discussion Because PBE/vdW-DF underestimates the gap, the molecular resonance lands on $E_F$ and the junction transmits \"too well.\" Correcting the level alignment (B3LYP via a DFT+Σ shift) is what decides whether the headline picture is resonant or non-resonant — the computed shifts and the scattering-region length convergence are on the level correction page."}, {"p": "results/transmission/index.html", "t": "06. Transmission — the molecule is the conduction bridge", "x": "06. Transmission — the molecule is the conduction bridge 2026-06-12 – 06-19 · Zero-bias transport: pristine convergence · three-way · eigenchannel · I–V (TranSIESTA/TBtrans, vdW-DF2, k=100, η=0.005 eV) Goal Show whether current flows through the molecule, through which channel, and what the zero-bias I–V looks like. Method TranSIESTA / TBtrans, vdW-DF2, ~220-atom device, electrode $k=100$, $\\eta=0.005$ eV. $T(E)$ extracted with sisl from trans.TBT.nc ; eigenchannels from spectral-function decomposition at $E_F$, HOMO, and LUMO. Device structure The full ~220-atom scattering region — two VSe₃ leads with the Hf₂Se₉ molecule at the centre (drag to rotate, scroll to zoom): Result A. Pristine VSe₃ — length dependence is an η artifact For a continuous chain, $T(E)$ must be independent of electrode $k$-grid and scattering-region length. At $\\eta=0.05$ eV the broadening makes $T(E)$ length-dependent (N28 vs N38); at $\\eta=0.001$ eV it returns to length-independent — the dependence is a broadening artifact, not physics. Fig. 1. Pristine VSe₃ $T(E)$: $\\eta=0.05$ eV (left, length-dependent) vs $\\eta=0.001$ eV (right, length-independent). B. Three-way comparison — the molecule is the sole bridge System $T(E_F)$ Reading Pristine VSe₃ 0.704 reference (continuous chain) Heterojunction 0.348 conducts through the molecule Disconnected 0.000 molecule removed → no conduction Fig. 2. $T(E)$ — pristine VSe₃ (η = 0.001) / Hf₂Se₉ hetero / disconnected ($T\\approx0$, off scale; hetero·disc η = 0.005). Removing the molecule (~14 Å gap) kills conduction. The transmitting states are molecular: the Hf₂Se₉ frontier (HOMO/LUMO, near-degenerate and pinned at $E_F$ — isolated-molecule HOMO $=-0.020$, LUMO $=+0.003$ eV, gap $0.023$ eV) sits right at $E_F$, so the molecular PDOS peaks there ($-0.065$ / $0$ eV) and the $T(E)$ peaks line up. The $+0.175$ eV peak is a higher unoccupied molecular resonance at $+0.15$ eV (isolated MO87/88), well above the $E_F$-pinned frontier manifold (MO82–86) — not the LUMO. Fig. 3. Heterojunction $T(E)$ (black) with total DOS (gray) and molecular Hf₂Se₉ PDOS (red). Molecular resonances near $E_F$ carry the transmission. C. Eigenchannel — the channel runs through the molecule Real-space $|\\psi|^2$ isosurface of the transmission eigenchannel (from the EC_0_psi2_*.cube files; V grey, Se green). At $E_F$ the channel (EC₀, $\\tau=0.999$) runs along the whole device and clearly threads through the Hf₂Se₉ at the centre — direct real-space evidence of molecule-mediated conduction. (Renders below: 3-cell device N25_k100; the length-converged 9-cell device gives the same channel structure, $E_F$ $\\tau_0 = 0.989$.) Fig. 4. $E_F$ eigenchannel EC₀ ($\\tau=0.999$) $|\\psi|^2$ isosurface — threads through the molecule (centre lobe) and into both VSe₃ leads. Fig. 5a. Channel at $-0.065$ eV (HOMO-like), zoomed to the molecule region. Fig. 5b. Channel at $+0.175$ eV (a higher molecular resonance, MO87/88 well above the $E_F$-pinned frontier — not the LUMO), zoomed to the molecule region. Note: the $+0.175$ eV level is a higher unoccupied molecular resonance (isolated MO87/88 at $+0.154$/$+0.156$ eV), well above the $E_F$-pinned frontier manifold, not the LUMO — the true HOMO/LUMO (MO83/84) are near-degenerate and pinned at $E_F$ (HOMO $-0.020$, LUMO $+0.003$ eV), which is why conduction is concentrated there. Each cube is ~45 MB ( N25_k100/0bias/eigenchannel/ ) — shown here as the rendered isosurface, not embedded raw. D. I–V from the zero-bias approximation Integrating the zero-bias $T(E)$ over the bias window (Landauer), without recomputing $T(E)$ at finite bias, gives the I–V. It is asymmetric (≈ $+5$ / $-2.5$ μA at $\\pm0.5$ V). Fig. 6a. Bias windows ($|V|=0.1$–$0.5$ V) over $T(E)$ — the integration ranges that give each current. Fig. 6b. Landauer I–V (zero-bias $T(E)$). Discussion Conduction is resonant because the PBE/vdW-DF molecular resonance sits on $E_F$. Whether it stays resonant under a corrected gap is the open question driving the B3LYP level correction ; the finite-bias I–V is the remaining deliverable."}, {"p": "results/vse3-tp-tap/index.html", "t": "01. VSe₃ TP vs TAP stability", "x": "01. VSe₃ TP vs TAP stability 2026-04-07 · Source: meetings 2026-03-19, 2026-04-07, 2026-04-16; initial-structures report. Goal Determine which 1D chain coordination — TP (trigonal prismatic) or TAP (trigonal anti-prismatic) — is more stable for VSe₃, supporting the experimental (STEM) claim that the CNT-confined VSe₃ single chain is TP. The two differ by a 60° rotation between adjacent Se₃ triangles along the chain axis: in TP the triangles are aligned (top view shows 3 Se), in TAP adjacent triangles are rotated 60° (top view shows a 6-pointed star). Method Initial structures were built from literature parameters with build_vse3.py : TP from d VSe = 2.45 Å, d VV = 3.09 Å (covalent-radii sum, Cordero 2008 + NbSe₃ scaling, Hodeau 1978); TAP is the same with a 60° rotation (c = 6.18 Å, 8 atoms). Both were relaxed in SIESTA. TP/TAP stability was then compared with two functionals, vdW-DF2 (LMKLL) and PBE+D2. Within a single functional the total-energy difference is a valid comparison; across functionals it is not (see stability methodology ). Result TP is more stable than TAP by 0.163 eV per formula unit (vdW-DF2 bulk relax, robust to k-mesh; ≈ 41 meV/atom). The TP band structure is metallic. Polytype E / formula (eV) V–V (Å) cell c (Å) TP (prismatic) −11957.142 3.116 3.116 TAP (antiprismatic) −11956.979 2.766 5.531 ΔE (TP − TAP) −0.163 — — TP keeps its upper/lower Se triangles aligned (3 Se overlap in top view); TAP is the 60°-rotated (octahedral) stacking, metallic in both functionals. (Source: 2026-06-12 meeting.) Fig. 1. TP (left) vs TAP (right); top views (above, along the chain axis) and side views (below). TP keeps its Se₃ triangles aligned (3 Se overlap); TAP is the 60°-rotated stacking. Interactive — relaxed chains (drag to rotate, scroll to zoom): TP (left), TAP (right): TP TAP Fig. 2. TP single chain (V–V = 3.116 Å) and its band structure (−Z–Γ–Z): several bands cross $E_F$ → metallic. Discussion The TAP relaxed structure was delivered to the experimental team (Dr. Yangjin Lee) for STEM image simulation (2026-04-16). Its vdW-DF2 relaxed local parameters: c = 5.535 Å, V–Se = 2.508 Å, V–V = 2.768 Å. A planned extension is a Se-vs-Te (VSe₃ vs VTe₃) TP/TAP comparison; VTe₃ TP/TAP initial structures were built (cases 03/04) but those relaxations are listed as 2nd priority and not reported here."}, {"p": "status/index.html", "t": "Status working", "x": "Status working Current snapshot first, then the dated progress timeline Now (transport phase) Phase: the structure is settled, the transport is converged, and the DFT+Σ molecular-level correction is applied ($T(E_F)\\,0.355\\to0.051$ on the 9-cell device). Queued: the true finite-bias chain (corrected $\\Delta H$) and the interface charge transfer $\\Delta\\rho$. Settled conclusions Functional: production = vdW-DF2 (PBE+D2 anomalous — dropped). Geometry: 60° staggered interface most stable (−42 meV, cell-fixed); H-passivated, length-converged scattering region. Molecule is the conduction bridge: $T(E_F)$ = 0.70 / 0.35 / 0 for pristine / heterojunction / disconnected (k = 100, η = 0.005 eV); transmission eigenchannel $\\tau_0\\approx1$. Length-independent: $T(E_F)$ = 0.43 / 0.35 / 0.29 across three scattering-region lengths — essentially flat. Molecular frontier: Hf₂Se₉ HOMO/LUMO quasi-degenerate, pinned at $E_F$ (gap ≈ 0.024 eV, vdW-DF2); the Hf₂Se₉ chains are metallic. DFT+Σ correction applied (2026-07-01/02): $T(E_F)\\,0.355\\to0.051$, molecular PDOS$(E_F)\\,20\\to1.2$/eV; the transmission eigenchannel moves to the HOMO resonance ($-0.095$ eV, $\\tau_0=0.373$). Corrected Landauer I–V (2026-07-02): the correction suppresses the low-bias current and strengthens the rectification — ±0.5 V $+5.04/-2.70\\to+4.30/-1.04$ μA, $|I_+/I_-|$ 1.9 → 4.1. In progress Planned: true finite-bias chain (0 → ±0.5 V, corrected $\\Delta H$) and interface charge transfer $\\Delta\\rho$. Progress timeline What was added / settled, by date. Structure phase → transport phase. The full frozen structure-phase site is under Iterations . 2026-03 – 04-07 · structure & stability VSe₃ TP vs TAP: TP consistently more stable ; Hf₂Se₉ molecule/chain (vdW-DF2) relaxed — over-expansion confirmed; hetero separation monotonic. Work package Status Comment VSe₃ TP/TAP comparison Done TP consistently more stable Hf₂Se₉ mol/chain (vdW-DF2) Done Over-expansion confirmed Phase A functional scan Closed Production functional settled to vdW-DF2 Hetero separation test Done All 3 versions monotonically decreasing CNT stabilization study Shelved 2026-04-07 meeting decision Phase A — Hf–Hf vs experiment (TEM 3.6 Å): Test Method Code Hf–Hf (Å) Δ(3.6 Å) Status A1 PBE SIESTA 3.857 +7% ✅ A2 PBE+D2 SIESTA — — ❌ SCF not converged A3 vdW-DF2 (LMKLL) SIESTA 3.967 +10% ✅ A4 PBE+D3(BJ) SIESTA 3.857 +7% ✅ A5 PBE+D3 VASP 2.934 −18.5% ✅ A6 HSE06+D3 VASP 3.330 −7.5% ✅ 2026-04-16 · H termination & interface H termination restores a separation-energy minimum. bond/gap/tilt modes compared (vdW-DF2, 44 atoms, Se–H = 1.46 Å); gap most stable, minimum at d = 3.5–4.0 Å ( bond +1.11 eV, tilt +3.72 eV). See H termination . VSe₃ TAP relaxed structure delivered to the experimental team (STEM simulation): c = 5.535 Å, V–Se = 2.508 Å, V–V = 2.768 Å. 2026-06-02 · η-broadening resolved The apparent length dependence of pristine $T(E)$ is an η-broadening artifact (η 0.05 → 0.001 eV restores length independence), not physics; TBtrans contour method + η fixed → production η = 0.005 eV . 2026-06-12 · junction confirmed, transport established Structure confirmed: Hf₂Se₉ relaxes cleanly under vdW-DF2 (D2 dropped); eigenchannel crosses the molecule (connected, not tunnelling). Three-way comparison pristine / heterojunction / disconnected → molecule is the sole conduction bridge. Electrode k-grid 4 → 100 converged ($T(E_F)$ shift ~0.3%); B3LYP vacuum shift computed: $\\Delta_\\text{HOMO}=-1.015$, $\\Delta_\\text{LUMO}=-0.286$ eV. 2026-06-19 · convergence checks done, correction designed Convergence checks (scattering-region length, electrode k-grid, η, benchmarks) done; B3LYP molecular gap = 0.752 eV . Advisor derived the $\\Delta H$ correction (DFT+Σ) on the board → implement via TBtrans δH (no source patch); SCF wall-time 18 h → 9 h via mixer tuning. Re-relaxation at ≥ 5 (ideally 9) VSe₃ unit cells per electrode assigned. 2026-06-26 · length convergence + correction inputs Scattering region extended to 9 VSe₃ unit cells per electrode: $T(E_F)$ = 0.348 ≈ 0.354 (3 cells), single transmission eigenchannel ($\\tau_0 = 0.989$); interface geometry and molecular PDOS length-independent. See level correction . B3LYP→vdW-DF2 orbital shifts computed ($\\Delta_\\text{HOMO} = -1.01$, $\\Delta_\\text{LUMO} = -0.29$ eV; gap 0.02 → 0.75 eV) — DFT+Σ application is the next step. 2026-07-01 – 07-02 · correction applied; three-way + corrected I–V complete DFT+Σ applied on the 9-cell device ($T(E_F)$ 0.355 → 0.051); disconnected device done → three-way comparison complete; corrected Landauer I–V posted (rectification 1.9 → 4.1)."}];
